export default {"cls": "mm","label": "Math Modeling","items": [{"id": "MM.1-01","std": "MM.1","q": "In the Mathematical Modeling Cycle, what is the first step when solving a real-world problem such as reducing school cafeteria food waste?","a": "Identify the problem and define the variables","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Identify the problem and define the variables","Verify the model against new data","Report the final results","Perform a regression analysis"],"steps": ["Think about what you must do before collecting data or doing math.","You first need to know what the problem is and what can change.","Match that starting step to the choices and check your pick."]},{"id": "MM.1-02","std": "MM.1","q": "A student models the monthly cost of running a lawn-mowing business. Which of the following is an assumption rather than a measured fact?","a": "Gas prices will remain at §3.50 per gallon all summer","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Each customer pays §35 per lawn","Gas prices will remain at §3.50 per gallon all summer","The student mowed 12 lawns last week","The lawn mower cost §420 at the store"],"steps": ["An assumption is a guess or condition you treat as true.","Measured facts come from prices paid or counts already made.","Find the choice about a future guess and check your answer."]},{"id": "MM.1-03","std": "MM.1","q": "In the Statistical Problem-Solving Cycle, after formulating a statistical question and collecting data, what is the next step?","a": "Analyze the data","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Analyze the data","Formulate a new question","Publish the conclusion","Discard outliers"],"steps": ["List the steps of the statistical cycle in order.","After you ask a question and gather data, you study the data.","Pick the step that means studying data and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.1-04","std": "MM.1","q": "A school club plans a car wash fundraiser. Supplies cost §60, and they charge §10 per car. Their initial model for profit is P = 10c − 60. If they wash 25 cars, what is the predicted profit?","a": "§190","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§150","§310","§250","§190"],"steps": ["Plug the number of cars into the profit equation.","Replace c with 25 to get P = 10 × 25 − 60.","Multiply first, then subtract, and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.1-05","std": "MM.1","q": "A student models daily water usage in a household as W = 50p + 30, where p is the number of people and W is gallons per day. A 4-person household actually uses 280 gallons per day. What is the difference between the actual usage and the model's prediction?","a": "50 gallons","lvl": 2,"choices": ["230 gallons","30 gallons","50 gallons","80 gallons"],"steps": ["Find the model's prediction for 4 people, then compare to actual.","Compute W = 50 × 4 + 30, then subtract that from 280.","Finish the subtraction and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.1-06","std": "MM.1","q": "A city models park attendance with A = 120 + 15t, where t is temperature in °F above 60°F. When the temperature is 80°F, attendance is predicted to be 420. If rain reduces attendance by 40%, what is the revised prediction?","a": "252 people","lvl": 2,"choices": ["168 people","288 people","380 people","252 people"],"steps": ["A 40% drop leaves 60% of the predicted attendance.","Set up 420 × 0.60, or find 40% of 420 and subtract it.","Finish the calculation and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.1-07","std": "MM.1","q": "A student's model for coffee shop revenue is R = 4.50c, where c is cups sold. After testing the model for a week, actual revenue is consistently 15% higher than predicted because customers also buy pastries. How should the student revise the model in the next iteration of the modeling cycle?","a": "Add a variable or average pastry revenue per customer to the equation","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Add a variable or average pastry revenue per customer to the equation","Stop the modeling cycle because the error is under 20%","Ignore pastry sales because they were not in the original plan","Change the price of coffee in the model to §4.50"],"steps": ["Ask why actual revenue is always higher than the model predicts.","A good model update includes the missing source of revenue.","Choose the revision that adds pastry sales and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.1-08","std": "MM.1","q": "Two models predict monthly electric bills (y) from square footage (x): Model A is y = 0.12x + 25 and Model B is y = 0.10x + 65. For a 1,500 sq ft home with an actual bill of §210, which model is more accurate and by how much?","a": "Both models have the exact same error","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Both models have the exact same error","Model B is more accurate by §5","Model A is more accurate by §5","Model A is more accurate by §10"],"steps": ["Plug x = 1,500 into both equations to get each predicted bill.","Find each model's error by taking the gap from §210.","Compare the two errors, find the difference, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.1-09","std": "MM.1","q": "A school asks if roof solar panels pay off over 15 years. Which plan follows the Mathematical Modeling Cycle?","a": "Define cost and energy variables, assume a fixed rate, build, test, refine","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Install the panels first, then see if they pay off","Define cost and energy variables, assume a fixed rate, build, test, refine","Pick the cheapest panels without building a model","Look up one neighbor's electric bill and decide"],"steps": ["List four stages: define, assume, build a model, and test or refine.","Pick two key cost or energy variables and one simple assumption.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.2-01","std": "MM.2","q": "Maya earns §2,400 per month after taxes. Her monthly expenses total §1,950. How much money can she save each month?","a": "§450","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§550","§350","§4,350","§450"],"steps": ["Savings equal income minus expenses.","Set up the subtraction: §2,400 − §1,950.","Subtract carefully and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.2-02","std": "MM.2","q": "Devon wants an emergency fund equal to 3 months of essential expenses. His essential expenses are §1,600 per month. What is his target emergency fund?","a": "§4,800","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§6,400","§5,400","§4,800","§3,200"],"steps": ["Multiply one month of expenses by the number of months.","Set up the product: §1,600 × 3.","Find the total and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.2-03","std": "MM.2","q": "A part-time job pays §16 per hour. If Jordan works 20 hours per week and 15% is withheld for taxes, what is Jordan's weekly take-home pay?","a": "§272","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§320","§272","§48","§288"],"steps": ["First find gross pay, then keep the 85% left after taxes.","Multiply §16 × 20, then multiply that gross pay by 0.85.","Finish multiplying and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.2-04","std": "MM.2","q": "Elena has §600 saved and plans to save §225 each month for a §2,400 used car. How many months will it take her to reach her goal?","a": "8 months","lvl": 2,"choices": ["11 months","6 months","8 months","10 months"],"steps": ["Find how much more money Elena still needs to save.","Subtract §2,400 − §600, then divide that amount by §225.","Finish dividing and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.2-05","std": "MM.2","q": "Three roommates split §1,800 in rent and §240 in utilities equally. If one roommate also pays §60 for internet alone, what is that roommate's total monthly housing cost?","a": "§740","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§680","§740","§700","§660"],"steps": ["Add the shared bills first, split 3 ways, then add internet.","Set up (§1,800 + §240) ÷ 3 + §60.","Finish the operations in order and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.2-06","std": "MM.2","q": "A freelance designer charges §50 per hour, sets aside 25% of gross income for taxes, and pays §150 per month for software. If she works 40 hours in a month, what is her net income?","a": "§1,350","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§1,350","§1,500","§1,850","§1,387.50"],"steps": ["Find gross pay, take out 25% for taxes, then subtract software.","Compute §50 × 40 × 0.75, then subtract §150.","Finish simplifying and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.2-07","std": "MM.2","q": "Job A pays §3,200/month after taxes with a §280/month commute cost. Job B pays §3,050/month after taxes and is remote (no commute cost), but requires §60/month in upgraded internet. Which job yields more net monthly income and by how much?","a": "Job B by §70 per month","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Job B by §70 per month","Job B by §130 per month","Job A by §150 per month","Job A by §70 per month"],"steps": ["Find net monthly income for each job by subtracting its extra cost.","Compare (§3,200 − §280) with (§3,050 − §60).","Find the difference between the two nets and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.2-08","std": "MM.2","q": "Carlos budgets §2,800/month: 40% for rent, 20% for food, 25% for other expenses, and 15% for savings. Next year his rent increases by 10% while his income stays the same. If he keeps food and other expenses unchanged, how much will he have left for monthly savings?","a": "§308","lvl": 3,"choices": ["§140","§420","§378","§308"],"steps": ["Find the original rent and savings, or find how much rent rises.","Find 10% of the 40% rent share and subtract that increase from savings.","Finish the subtraction and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.2-09","std": "MM.2","q": "A graduate earns §3,500 a month after taxes and wants to save §6,000 in 12 months while paying §1,200 rent, §350 student loans and §450 food. Is the goal reachable?","a": "Yes: needs §500 a month and leaves §1,000 for other costs","lvl": 3,"choices": ["No: needs §600 a month, but only §500 is left","Yes: needs §500 a month and leaves §2,000 for other costs","No: the fixed costs are more than the income","Yes: needs §500 a month and leaves §1,000 for other costs"],"steps": ["Find the monthly savings needed by dividing §6,000 by 12.","Subtract all listed bills and monthly savings from §3,500.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.3-01","std": "MM.3","q": "An account starts with §2,000 and earns 5% simple interest per year. What is the balance after 3 years?","a": "§2,300","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§2,300","§2,315.25","§2,150","§2,100"],"steps": ["Simple interest uses I = P × r × t, then add I to P.","Find interest with §2,000 × 0.05 × 3, then add §2,000.","Add carefully and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.3-02","std": "MM.3","q": "A delivery van is purchased for §24,000 and depreciates by §3,000 per year using straight-line depreciation. What is its value after 4 years?","a": "§12,000","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§18,000","§9,000","§12,000","§15,000"],"steps": ["Straight-line depreciation subtracts the same amount each year.","Set up §24,000 − (§3,000 × 4).","Multiply first, then subtract, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.3-03","std": "MM.3","q": "A bond's value each year forms the arithmetic sequence §500, §540, §580, §620, ... What is the annual simple interest earned?","a": "§40","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§80","§540","§40","§20"],"steps": ["In an arithmetic sequence, find the constant change between terms.","Subtract two neighboring terms, like §540 − §500.","Verify the same jump works for all terms and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.3-04","std": "MM.3","q": "A commercial printer costs §1,800 and has a salvage value of §200 after 8 years of straight-line depreciation. What is its value after 5 years?","a": "§800","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§1,000","§600","§675","§800"],"steps": ["First find the yearly depreciation, then subtract 5 years of it.","Find (§1,800 − §200) ÷ 8 per year, then subtract 5 times that from §1,800.","Finish the calculation and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.3-05","std": "MM.3","q": "A small business borrows §4,500 at 6% annual simple interest for 4 years. What is the total amount to be repaid at the end of 4 years?","a": "§5,580","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§5,681.15","§5,580","§1,080","§4,770"],"steps": ["Find total simple interest over 4 years, then add the loan amount.","Set up §4,500 + (§4,500 × 0.06 × 4).","Finish computing the total and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.3-06","std": "MM.3","q": "A farm tractor purchased for §35,000 depreciates by §2,500 each year. After how many years will its book value be §15,000?","a": "8 years","lvl": 2,"choices": ["6 years","10 years","14 years","8 years"],"steps": ["Find the total value lost, then divide by the yearly drop.","Set up (§35,000 − §15,000) ÷ §2,500.","Divide to find the years and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.3-07","std": "MM.3","q": "Bond X invests §8,000 at 4.5% simple interest per year. Bond Y invests §6,000 at 6% simple interest per year. After 5 years, how do their total values compare?","a": "Bond X is worth §2,000 more than Bond Y","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Bond X is worth §1,800 more than Bond Y","Bond Y is worth more than Bond X","They have the exact same value","Bond X is worth §2,000 more than Bond Y"],"steps": ["Compute the ending value of each bond using simple interest.","Use A = P + P × r × t for both Bond X and Bond Y with t = 5.","Compare the two final values and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.3-08","std": "MM.3","q": "A bakery buys an oven for §12,000 and depreciates it using straight-line depreciation to a salvage value of §2,400 over 6 years. If the bakery sells the oven after 3 years for §6,500, how does the sale price compare to its book value?","a": "§700 less than book value","lvl": 3,"choices": ["§500 more than book value","§700 more than book value","§700 less than book value","§1,600 less than book value"],"steps": ["Find the yearly drop, then find the book value after 3 years.","Compute yearly loss from (§12,000 − §2,400) ÷ 6, then compare to §6,500.","Find if §6,500 is above or below book value and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.3-09","std": "MM.3","q": "A §10,000 loan earns 5% simple interest per year, and a §10,000 machine loses §1,250 per year. Which explicit formulas model them?","a": "Loan: A(n) = 10000 + 500n; machine: V(n) = 10000 − 1250n","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Loan: A(n) = 10000(1.05)^n; machine: V(n) = 10000(0.875)^n","Loan: A(n) = 500n; machine: V(n) = 1250n","Loan: A(n) = 10000 + 5n; machine: V(n) = 10000 − 1250n","Loan: A(n) = 10000 + 500n; machine: V(n) = 10000 − 1250n"],"steps": ["Connect constant dollar change per year to a common difference.","Use start + or − (yearly change) × n for each formula.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.4-01","std": "MM.4","q": "You deposit §1,000 in an account earning 10% interest compounded annually. What is the balance after 2 years?","a": "§1,210","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§1,200","§1,210","§1,220","§1,100"],"steps": ["Compound interest multiplies by (1 + r) each year.","Set up A = §1,000 × (1.10)².","Evaluate the power, multiply, and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.4-02","std": "MM.4","q": "A laptop costs §1,000 and loses 20% of its value each year. What is its value after 2 years?","a": "§640","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§800","§400","§600","§640"],"steps": ["Losing 20% each year means keeping 80%, or 0.80, each year.","Set up V = §1,000 × (0.80)².","Square 0.80, multiply by 1,000, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.4-03","std": "MM.4","part": "a","q": "A savings account has an APR of 6% compounded once per year. What is its APY?","a": "6%","lvl": 1,"choices": ["12%","6%","0.5%","6.17%"],"steps": ["APY is the effective rate earned over one full year.","With compounding once per year, use (1 + 0.06)¹ − 1.","Convert back to a percent and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.4-04","std": "MM.4","q": "An investment of §5,000 earns 4% APR compounded quarterly. What is the balance after 3 years?","a": "§5,634.13","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§5,600.00","§5,624.32","§5,637.48","§5,634.13"],"steps": ["Use A = P(1 + {r/n})^{nt} with n = 4 quarters per year.","Plug in P = 5,000, r = 0.04, n = 4, and nt = 12.","Evaluate with a calculator, round to cents, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.4-05","std": "MM.4","q": "An account with §2,000 earns 5% APR compounded continuously. Using A = Pe^{rt}, what is the balance after 4 years?","a": "§2,442.81","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§2,460.00","§2,442.81","§2,431.01","§2,400.00"],"steps": ["Plug principal, rate as a decimal, and time into A = Pe^{rt}.","Set up A = 2,000e^{0.05 × 4}.","Multiply the exponent first, compute, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.4-06","std": "MM.4","part": "a","q": "A credit union offers 12% APR compounded monthly. What is the APY rounded to the nearest hundredth of a percent?","a": "12.68%","lvl": 2,"choices": ["13.00%","12.68%","12.36%","12.00%"],"steps": ["Use APY = (1 + {r/n})^{n} − 1 with n = 12 months.","Plug in r = 0.12 and n = 12 to get (1 + 0.01)^{12} − 1.","Turn the decimal into a percent, round, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.4-07","std": "MM.4","q": "A new SUV costs §30,000 and depreciates by 15% per year. What is its value after 5 years, rounded to the nearest cent?","a": "§13,311.16","lvl": 3,"choices": ["§11,314.49","§7,500.00","§13,311.16","§15,660.19"],"steps": ["A 15% yearly drop gives a decay factor of 1 − 0.15.","Set up V = 30,000 × (0.85)⁵.","Evaluate, round to the nearest cent, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.4-08","std": "MM.4","part": "a","q": "Bank A offers 6% APR compounded monthly. Bank B offers 5.9% APR compounded continuously. Which bank has the higher APY and by how much (rounded to the nearest hundredth of a percent)?","a": "Bank A by 0.09%","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Bank A by 0.09%","Bank A by 0.10%","Bank B by 0.09%","Both have the same APY"],"steps": ["Convert both banks to APY so you can compare them fairly.","Compute (1 + {0.06/12})^{12} − 1 for A and e^{0.059} − 1 for B.","Subtract the rounded percents and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.4-09","std": "MM.4","part": "a","q": "Why is APY greater than APR when interest compounds more than once a year?","a": "Interest earned early in the year also earns interest later","lvl": 3,"choices": ["APY includes bank fees that APR leaves out","Interest earned early in the year also earns interest later","APY is always double the APR","APR is calculated with a larger principal"],"steps": ["Identify which rate is nominal (APR) and which is effective (APY).","Match APR to r in Pe^{rt} and APY to r in P(1 + r)^{t}.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.5-01","std": "MM.5","q": "You invest §1,000 at 10% for 2 years. How much more does annual compound interest earn than simple interest?","a": "§10","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§0","§10","§20","§100"],"steps": ["Find the ending balance for both simple and compound interest.","Compare §1,000 + (§1,000 × 0.10 × 2) with §1,000 × (1.10)².","Subtract the two balances and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.5-02","std": "MM.5","q": "A §10,000 machine is modeled by straight-line depreciation (§2,000/year) and proportional depreciation (20%/year). After 2 years, how much higher is the proportional depreciation value?","a": "§400","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§0","§800","§400","§1,600"],"steps": ["Find the 2-year value under each depreciation model.","Compare §10,000 − 2(§2,000) with §10,000 × (0.80)².","Find the difference between them and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.5-03","std": "MM.5","q": "For the same principal and positive annual interest rate over 10 years, which type of interest produces a larger ending balance?","a": "Compound interest","lvl": 1,"choices": ["It depends on the principal","Compound interest","Simple interest","Both produce the same balance"],"steps": ["Think about how interest grows over multiple years.","Compound interest earns interest on past interest, not just principal.","Pick the faster-growing type and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.5-04","std": "MM.5","q": "Compare §4,000 invested at 5% simple interest versus 5% interest compounded annually for 3 years. How much more does the compound account have?","a": "§30.50","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§630.50","§10.00","§60.00","§30.50"],"steps": ["Compute the 3-year balance for simple and for compound interest.","Compare §4,000 + (§4,000 × 0.05 × 3) with §4,000 × (1.05)³.","Subtract the smaller from the larger and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.5-05","std": "MM.5","q": "A §20,000 truck is modeled by straight-line depreciation of §3,000/year and proportional depreciation of 15%/year. After 3 years, what is the difference between the two values?","a": "§1,282.50","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§1,282.50","§900.00","§1,450.00","§3,000.00"],"steps": ["Find both 3-year truck values, then find how far apart they are.","Compare §20,000 − 3(§3,000) with §20,000 × (0.85)³.","Subtract to find the gap and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.5-06","std": "MM.5","q": "A §15,000 generator loses §3,000/year under straight-line depreciation and 20%/year under proportional depreciation. When does each model reach a value of §0?","a": "Straight-line reaches §0 in 5 years; proportional never reaches §0","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Both reach §0 in 5 years","Proportional reaches §0 in 5 years; straight-line never reaches §0","Straight-line reaches §0 in 5 years; proportional never reaches §0","Neither model reaches §0"],"steps": ["Test when each model can actually equal §0.","Solve 15,000 − 3,000t = 0 and check if 15,000 × (0.80)^{t} can be 0.","Match both behaviors to the choices and check your pick."]},{"id": "MM.5-07","std": "MM.5","q": "Account A invests §5,000 at 6% simple interest. Account B invests §5,000 at 5% interest compounded annually. After 10 years, which account has more money and by how much?","a": "Account B by §144.47","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Account B by §144.47","Account A by §500.00","Account B by §288.95","Account A by §144.47"],"steps": ["Find both 10-year balances using the right formula for each account.","Compare 5,000(1 + 0.06 × 10) against 5,000 × (1.05)^{10}.","Find which is larger, take the difference, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.5-08","std": "MM.5","q": "A §12,000 piece of equipment loses §2,000/year under straight-line depreciation and 20%/year under proportional depreciation. In which year does the proportional value first become greater than the straight-line value?","a": "Year 3","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Year 4","Year 2","Year 3","Year 1"],"steps": ["Make a year-by-year table for both models starting at Year 1.","Subtract §2,000 each year for one, and multiply by 0.80 for the other.","Find the first year proportional is higher and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.5-09","std": "MM.5","q": "Why does proportional depreciation fit vehicle resale better than straight-line depreciation?","a": "Cars lose the most value early and never fall to §0","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Cars gain value after the first year","Cars lose the same dollar amount every year","Cars lose the most value early and never fall to §0","Proportional depreciation is simpler for taxes"],"steps": ["Compare how each model changes dollar value from year to year.","Link equal yearly drops to taxes, and fast early drops to car resale.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.6-01","std": "MM.6","q": "A credit card has a beginning balance of §600. During the month, the holder makes a §150 payment, §100 in new purchases, and is charged §10 in interest. What is the ending balance?","a": "§560","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§660","§540","§560","§860"],"steps": ["Payments lower a credit card balance; purchases and interest raise it.","Set up §600 − §150 + §100 + §10.","Compute left to right and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.6-02","std": "MM.6","q": "A credit card has an APR of 18%. What is the monthly periodic interest rate?","a": "1.5%","lvl": 1,"choices": ["1.5%","0.15%","18%","1.8%"],"steps": ["APR is the yearly rate, and there are 12 months in a year.","Divide the APR by 12: 18% ÷ 12.","Finish dividing and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.6-03","std": "MM.6","q": "A credit card carries a §1,000 unpaid balance at a 2% monthly interest rate. What is the finance charge for the month?","a": "§20","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§20","§2","§24","§200"],"steps": ["Multiply the unpaid balance by the monthly interest rate.","Convert 2% to 0.02 and set up §1,000 × 0.02.","Finish multiplying and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.6-04","std": "MM.6","q": "In a credit card spreadsheet, beginning balance is §800, payment is §200, new purchases are §150, and a 1.5% monthly interest rate is charged on (beginning balance − payment). What is the ending balance?","a": "§759","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§764.25","§762","§759","§750"],"steps": ["Subtract the payment first, charge 1.5% on that, then add purchases.","Set up (§800 − §200) × 1.015 + §150.","Simplify step by step and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.6-05","std": "MM.6","q": "A credit card has a §1,200 balance at 24% APR (2% per month) with no new purchases. Each month, interest is added first and then a §200 payment is made. What is the balance after 2 months?","a": "§844.48","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§1,024.00","§844.48","§848.00","§800.00"],"steps": ["Track one month at a time: multiply by 1.02, then subtract §200.","Find Month 1's end balance, then repeat the same rule for Month 2.","Round to the nearest cent and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.6-06","std": "MM.6","q": "A cardholder with a §500 balance misses a payment and is charged a §35 late fee. Then 2% monthly interest is applied to the new total. What is the resulting balance?","a": "§545.70","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§535.00","§545.70","§510.00","§545.00"],"steps": ["Add the late fee first because interest applies to the new total.","Set up (§500 + §35) × 1.02.","Finish multiplying and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.6-07","std": "MM.6","q": "In a spreadsheet, B2 is beginning balance, C2 is payment, D2 is new purchases, and E2 is late fee. Interest of 1.5% is charged on (B2 − C2). Which formula correctly computes the ending balance in F2?","a": "=(B2-C2) × 1.015+D2+E2","lvl": 3,"choices": ["=(B2+D2-C2) × 0.015+E2","=(B2-C2) × 1.015+D2+E2","=(B2-C2+D2+E2) × 1.015","=B2 × 1.015-C2+D2+E2"],"steps": ["Match each cell to its role and note which part earns 1.5% interest.","Multiply only (B2 − C2) by 1.015, then add D2 and E2 outside.","Match that exact structure to the choices and check your pick."]},{"id": "MM.6-08","std": "MM.6","q": "A §2,000 credit card balance has a 24% APR (2% monthly). If the cardholder makes no new purchases and pays §40 each month, what happens to the balance over time?","a": "The balance stays at §2,000 forever because the payment only covers monthly interest","lvl": 3,"choices": ["The balance is paid off in 50 months","The balance drops by §40 every month","The balance grows by §40 every month","The balance stays at §2,000 forever because the payment only covers monthly interest"],"steps": ["Find one month's interest charge and compare it to the §40 payment.","Compute §2,000 × 0.02 to see how much of the payment lowers principal.","Decide what happens to the balance each month and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.6-09","std": "MM.6","q": "In a credit card payoff spreadsheet, why does paying §50 above the minimum cut total interest so much?","a": "The extra goes to principal, shrinking the balance that earns interest","lvl": 3,"choices": ["The bank waives interest on payments over the minimum","The extra goes to principal, shrinking the balance that earns interest","Extra payments only shorten the time, not the interest","It lowers the card's APR"],"steps": ["List the columns needed: Month, Start, Interest, Payment, and End.","Show how End = Start + Interest − Payment and carries to the next row.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.7-01","std": "MM.7","q": "An apartment rents for §1,200 per month plus §20 per month for renter's insurance. What is the total annual housing cost?","a": "§14,640","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§14,400","§14,420","§1,220","§14,640"],"steps": ["Add the two monthly costs, then multiply by 12 months.","Set up (§1,200 + §20) × 12.","Finish the math and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.7-02","std": "MM.7","part": "b","q": "A buyer purchases a §200,000 home with a 20% down payment. How much is the mortgage loan?","a": "§160,000","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§180,000","§240,000","§160,000","§40,000"],"steps": ["A 20% down payment leaves 80% of the price to borrow.","Set up §200,000 × 0.80, or subtract 20% from §200,000.","Finish computing the loan amount and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.7-03","std": "MM.7","part": "a","q": "What is the main advantage of a 30-year fixed-rate mortgage compared to an adjustable-rate mortgage (ARM)?","a": "The interest rate and monthly principal-and-interest payment stay the same for the entire loan","lvl": 1,"choices": ["The interest rate and monthly principal-and-interest payment stay the same for the entire loan","It pays off the home in half the time","It always has a lower initial interest rate","It requires no down payment"],"steps": ["Compare what 'fixed-rate' means versus 'adjustable-rate'.","A fixed rate never changes, so your main monthly payment stays steady.","Find the choice about predictable payments and check your pick."]},{"id": "MM.7-04","std": "MM.7","part": "b","q": "For the same loan amount, a 30-year mortgage has a §1,500 monthly payment and a 15-year mortgage has a §2,100 monthly payment. How much less is paid in total over the life of the 15-year loan?","a": "§162,000","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§600","§216,000","§108,000","§162,000"],"steps": ["Find the total paid over all months for each mortgage.","Compare §1,500 × 360 months with §2,100 × 180 months.","Subtract the two totals and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.7-05","std": "MM.7","q": "A lease-to-purchase contract charges §1,800/month for 24 months, with §300/month credited toward the down payment, plus an upfront §5,000 option fee also credited to the down payment. What total amount is credited toward the down payment after 24 months?","a": "§12,200","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§7,200","§48,200","§43,200","§12,200"],"steps": ["Only count the amounts that go toward the down payment.","Set up (§300 × 24) + §5,000.","Multiply first, add the fee, and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.7-06","std": "MM.7","part": "c","q": "Renting costs §1,400/month while owning a comparable home costs §1,900/month. If a renter invests the monthly savings, how much principal will they invest over 3 years (ignoring investment returns)?","a": "§18,000","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§18,000","§50,400","§15,000","§6,000"],"steps": ["Find the monthly savings, then multiply by the months in 3 years.","Set up (§1,900 − §1,400) × 36.","Finish multiplying and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.7-07","std": "MM.7","part": "b","q": "For a §300,000 home, Option A uses 10% down and a 30-year payment of §1,700/month. Option B uses 20% down and a 30-year payment of §1,440/month. Including the down payment, how much less total cash does Option B cost over 30 years?","a": "§63,600","lvl": 3,"choices": ["§30,000","§63,600","§33,600","§93,600"],"steps": ["Find total cash for each option: down payment plus 360 payments.","Compute Option A and Option B totals using 10% and 20% of §300,000.","Subtract Option B from Option A and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.7-08","std": "MM.7","part": "c","q": "A buyer purchases a §250,000 home with §50,000 down, and the home appreciates 3% per year for 5 years. A renter invests that §50,000 at 7% compounded annually for 5 years. Which grows by more dollars over 5 years, the home's value or the renter's investment?","a": "The home's value grows by §19,690.93 more","lvl": 3,"choices": ["The home's value grows by §39,818.52 more","The renter's investment grows by §19,690.93 more","Both grow by the same dollar amount","The home's value grows by §19,690.93 more"],"steps": ["Find the dollar gain (ending minus starting) for both options.","Use 250,000 × (1.03)⁵ − 250,000 and 50,000 × (1.07)⁵ − 50,000.","Compare the two dollar gains, take their gap, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.7-09","std": "MM.7","part": "a","q": "Compared with an FHA loan at 3.5% down, what is the main advantage and disadvantage of a conventional loan with 20% down?","a": "Advantage: no PMI. Disadvantage: much more cash needed up front","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Advantage: easier credit rules. Disadvantage: higher interest","Advantage: less cash up front. Disadvantage: mortgage insurance","Advantage: no interest. Disadvantage: shorter loan term","Advantage: no PMI. Disadvantage: much more cash needed up front"],"steps": ["Compare upfront cash needed, monthly payments, and mortgage insurance.","List one pro and one con for conventional, then one pro and con for FHA.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.8-01","std": "MM.8","q": "A car lease costs §300 per month for 36 months plus §2,000 due at signing. What is the total cost of the lease?","a": "§12,800","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§10,800","§12,800","§11,000","§14,800"],"steps": ["Add all monthly lease payments to the upfront signing cost.","Set up (§300 × 36) + §2,000.","Multiply first, then add, and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.8-02","std": "MM.8","q": "A used car is priced at §22,000. If you make a §4,000 down payment, how much will you finance with a loan?","a": "§18,000","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§18,000","§20,000","§16,000","§26,000"],"steps": ["The financed loan amount is the price minus the down payment.","Set up §22,000 − §4,000.","Subtract carefully and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.8-03","std": "MM.8","q": "An auto loan requires monthly payments of §420 for 60 months. What is the total of all monthly payments?","a": "§25,200","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§24,000","§25,200","§21,000","§25,620"],"steps": ["Multiply the monthly payment by the number of months.","Set up §420 × 60.","Find the total and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.8-04","std": "MM.8","q": "A 3-year car lease allows 12,000 miles per year and charges §0.25 per excess mile. If the driver puts 40,000 miles on the car over 3 years, what is the excess mileage fee?","a": "§1,000","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§400","§1,600","§1,000","§7,000"],"steps": ["Find allowed miles for 3 years, then find the extra miles driven.","Multiply (40,000 − 12,000 × 3) by §0.25.","Finish the math and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.8-05","std": "MM.8","q": "A car can be bought for §25,000 cash or financed with §3,000 down and §450/month for 60 months. How much extra does financing cost compared to paying cash?","a": "§5,000","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§2,000","§8,000","§3,000","§5,000"],"steps": ["Find the total financed cost, then subtract the §25,000 cash price.","Set up §3,000 + (§450 × 60) − §25,000.","Simplify in order and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.8-06","std": "MM.8","q": "Buying a car for §28,000 cash and selling it after 3 years for §17,000 has a net cost of §11,000. Leasing the same car for 36 months costs §320/month plus §2,500 due at signing. How much does buying with cash save over 3 years?","a": "§3,020","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§2,500","§520","§3,020","§14,020"],"steps": ["Find the total 3-year lease cost, then compare it to §11,000.","Compute (§320 × 36) + §2,500, then subtract §11,000.","Finish the subtraction and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.8-07","std": "MM.8","q": "To finance §20,000, Loan A is 48 months at §460/month and Loan B is 72 months at §330/month. Compare the monthly payment and total cost of Loan B to Loan A.","a": "Loan B is §130/month cheaper but costs §1,680 more in total","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Loan B is §130/month cheaper but costs §3,760 more in total","Loan A and Loan B have the same total cost","Loan B is §130/month cheaper but costs §1,680 more in total","Loan B is §130/month cheaper and costs §1,680 less in total"],"steps": ["Compare both the monthly payments and the full loan totals.","Find §460 − §330 per month, then compare (48 × §460) and (72 × §330).","Find the total cost gap and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.8-08","std": "MM.8","q": "Option 1: Lease a car for §350/month for 36 months with §2,000 down, then buy it at lease-end for §15,000 cash. Option 2: Buy it immediately with §4,000 down and §500/month for 60 months. Which option costs less in total and by how much?","a": "Option 1 costs §4,400 less","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Option 2 costs §1,400 less","Option 1 costs §4,400 less","Option 2 costs §4,400 less","Option 1 costs §2,400 less"],"steps": ["Add every payment and lump sum for Option 1 and for Option 2.","Compare (§350 × 36 + §2,000 + §15,000) with (§4,000 + §500 × 60).","Find which total is lower, subtract, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.8-09","std": "MM.8","q": "A sales rep drives 18,000 miles a year and will keep a car 7 years. Which option fits best?","a": "Buy, with cash or a low-rate loan; a lease's mileage limits cost too much","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Lease a new car every year to avoid repairs","Lease, because the rep builds equity in the car","Lease, because leases have no mileage limits","Buy, with cash or a low-rate loan; a lease's mileage limits cost too much"],"steps": ["Check how 18,000 miles per year fits typical lease mileage limits.","Weigh owning equity over 7 years against loan interest and cash use.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.9-01","std": "MM.9","q": "A rectangular shipping box is 10 inches long, 6 inches wide, and 4 inches tall. What is its volume?","a": "240 cubic inches","lvl": 1,"choices": ["240 cubic inches","20 cubic inches","120 cubic inches","248 cubic inches"],"steps": ["Volume of a rectangular prism is length × width × height.","Set up V = 10 × 6 × 4.","Multiply all three dimensions and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.9-02","std": "MM.9","q": "A cylindrical soup can has a radius of 3 cm and a height of 10 cm. What is its volume rounded to the nearest hundredth?","a": "282.74 cm³","lvl": 1,"choices": ["282.74 cm³","94.25 cm³","314.16 cm³","188.50 cm³"],"steps": ["Use the cylinder volume formula V = π × r² × h.","Plug in r = 3 and h = 10 to get V = π × 3² × 10.","Multiply, round to the nearest hundredth, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.9-03","std": "MM.9","q": "A cubic storage ottoman has an edge length of 5 cm in a scale prototype. What is its total surface area?","a": "150 cm²","lvl": 1,"choices": ["150 cm²","100 cm²","125 cm²","25 cm²"],"steps": ["A cube has 6 equal square faces, so SA = 6 × s².","Plug in s = 5 to set up SA = 6 × 5².","Square 5 first, multiply by 6, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.9-04","std": "MM.9","q": "A shipping carton must hold exactly 24 cubic blocks, each with an edge length of 3 inches. What is the minimum interior volume of the carton?","a": "648 cubic inches","lvl": 2,"choices": ["1,728 cubic inches","216 cubic inches","72 cubic inches","648 cubic inches"],"steps": ["Find the volume of one cube, then multiply by 24 blocks.","Set up V = 24 × 3³.","Evaluate the power, multiply, and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.9-05","std": "MM.9","q": "A carpenter builds an open-top rectangular wooden planter that is 4 ft long, 2 ft wide, and 1.5 ft tall. How many square feet of wood are needed for the bottom and four sides?","a": "26 sq ft","lvl": 2,"choices": ["26 sq ft","34 sq ft","12 sq ft","18 sq ft"],"steps": ["An open-top box has 1 bottom face and 4 side faces.","Set up (4 × 2) + 2(4 × 1.5) + 2(2 × 1.5).","Find each area, add them up, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.9-06","std": "MM.9","q": "A cylindrical rainwater tank has a diameter of 6 ft and a height of 8 ft. What is its capacity in cubic feet, rounded to the nearest hundredth?","a": "226.19 ft³","lvl": 2,"choices": ["452.39 ft³","150.80 ft³","904.78 ft³","226.19 ft³"],"steps": ["Divide the diameter by 2 first to get the radius.","Use V = π × r² × h with r = 6 ÷ 2 and h = 8.","Compute, round to the nearest hundredth, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.9-07","std": "MM.9","q": "A company compares two closed cardboard boxes that both hold 64 cubic inches: Box A is a 4 in x 4 in x 4 in cube, and Box B is an 8 in x 4 in x 2 in prism. Which box uses less cardboard and by how much?","a": "Box A uses 16 sq in less cardboard","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Box A uses 32 sq in less cardboard","Box B uses 16 sq in less cardboard","Both use the same amount of cardboard","Box A uses 16 sq in less cardboard"],"steps": ["Compare total surface area of both closed boxes.","Use SA = 2(lw + lh + wh) for Box A and Box B.","Find which surface area is smaller, subtract, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.9-08","std": "MM.9","q": "A wheelchair ramp must rise 2 feet with a maximum slope of 1:12 (1 foot of rise for every 12 feet of horizontal run). What is the minimum sloped length of the ramp, rounded to the nearest hundredth of a foot?","a": "24.08 ft","lvl": 3,"choices": ["24.08 ft","26.00 ft","24.00 ft","12.17 ft"],"steps": ["Use the 1:12 slope ratio to find the horizontal run for a 2 ft rise.","Apply the Pythagorean theorem to the rise and run: √{rise² + run²}.","Evaluate the root, round to hundredths, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.9-09","std": "MM.9","q": "A closed cylindrical can must hold 1,000 cm³ with the least metal. Which surface area function should you minimize?","a": "SA = 2πr² + 2000/r, from h = 1000/(πr²)","lvl": 3,"choices": ["SA = πr² × h = 1000","SA = 2πr² + 2000/r, from h = 1000/(πr²)","SA = 2πr² + 1000/r, from h = 1000/r","SA = 2πr + 2000/r², from h = 1000/r²"],"steps": ["Write V = π × r² × h = 1,000 and solve it for h in terms of r.","Substitute h into SA = 2π × r² + 2π × r × h.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.10-01","std": "MM.10","part": "a","q": "Which tool and level of precision is most appropriate for measuring a classroom's wall length to draw a 2D floor plan?","a": "A tape measure to the nearest 1/4 inch","lvl": 1,"choices": ["A car odometer to the nearest 0.1 mile","A 6-inch ruler to the nearest inch","A tape measure to the nearest 1/4 inch","A micrometer to the nearest 0.01 mm"],"steps": ["Match the measuring tool to the size of a classroom wall.","A room needs a tool that measures many feet to small fractions.","Pick the most practical tool and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.10-02","std": "MM.10","part": "b","q": "A rectangular shed is 12 ft wide with 8 ft vertical walls and a triangular gable roof that rises 4 ft above the walls. In a front elevation drawing, what is the total height from the ground to the roof peak?","a": "12 ft","lvl": 1,"choices": ["8 ft","12 ft","16 ft","24 ft"],"steps": ["Total height from ground to peak adds wall height and roof rise.","Set up 8 + 4 for the front elevation height.","Add the heights and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.10-03","std": "MM.10","part": "d","q": "An architect needs to give a builder exact wall heights, window placements, and roof slopes for a house. Which 2D visual representation is most appropriate?","a": "Scaled orthographic elevation drawings","lvl": 1,"choices": ["A scatter plot","A freehand perspective sketch","A pie chart","Scaled orthographic elevation drawings"],"steps": ["Think about which 2D drawing shows exact heights and slopes.","Builders use scaled straight-on side or front views, not sketches.","Pick the formal drawing type and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.10-04","std": "MM.10","part": "c","q": "A symmetrical gable roof spans a 16 ft wide building (8 ft from wall to center) and rises 6 ft from the wall top to the ridge. What is the slanted length of each rafter?","a": "10 ft","lvl": 2,"choices": ["12 ft","17.09 ft","10 ft","14 ft"],"steps": ["The half-span, rise, and rafter form a right triangle.","Use the Pythagorean theorem: rafter = √{8² + 6²}.","Simplify inside the root, take the root, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.10-05","std": "MM.10","part": "b","q": "An L-shaped building has a front wing 20 ft wide and 10 ft tall. Attached to its right side is a taller wing 10 ft wide and 15 ft tall. What are the overall width and maximum height shown in the front elevation drawing?","a": "30 ft wide and 15 ft tall","lvl": 2,"choices": ["30 ft wide and 25 ft tall","20 ft wide and 10 ft tall","20 ft wide and 15 ft tall","30 ft wide and 15 ft tall"],"steps": ["A front elevation shows total side-to-side width and tallest height.","Add widths (20 + 10) and take the taller of 10 ft and 15 ft.","Combine both measures and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.10-06","std": "MM.10","part": "c","q": "To include a tree in a site elevation drawing, a student measures its shadow as 15 ft at the same time a 6 ft fence post casts a 3 ft shadow. What is the calculated height of the tree?","a": "30 ft","lvl": 2,"choices": ["45 ft","7.5 ft","30 ft","18 ft"],"steps": ["Use similar triangles to set up a proportion of height to shadow.","Set up {h/15} = {6/3} and solve for h.","Finish solving for h and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.10-07","std": "MM.10","part": "c","q": "A surveyor stands 40 ft from a flagpole and measures an angle of elevation of 35° to the top from an eye height of 5 ft. What is the calculated height of the flagpole, rounded to the nearest hundredth?","a": "33.01 ft","lvl": 3,"choices": ["33.01 ft","37.77 ft","28.01 ft","62.13 ft"],"steps": ["Use tangent to find the height above eye level, then add 5 ft.","Set up height = 40tan(35°) + 5.","Compute in degree mode, round to hundredths, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.10-08","std": "MM.10","part": "a","q": "A rectangular patio measures 40 ft by 25 ft. If you draw a 2D plan view using a scale of 1 inch = 4 feet, what are the dimensions of the patio on paper?","a": "10 in by 6.25 in","lvl": 3,"choices": ["10 in by 6.25 in","8 in by 5 in","10 in by 5 in","160 in by 100 in"],"steps": ["Divide each real length by 4 feet per inch to get paper inches.","Set up {40/4} and {25/4} for the scaled dimensions.","Simplify both fractions and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.10-09","std": "MM.10","part": "d","q": "Which gives one advantage each for paper orthographic views and 3D CAD software when designing a bookshelf?","a": "Paper: fast and cheap. CAD: easy edits, exact dimensions, fit checks","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Paper: fast and cheap. CAD: easy edits, exact dimensions, fit checks","Both: give the same advantages, so neither is better","Paper: automatic dimensions. CAD: needs no computer","Paper: rotates the model. CAD: cheaper than paper"],"steps": ["Think about speed and cost for paper versus accuracy and edits for CAD.","Give one clear strength for paper views and one for 3D CAD.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.11-01","std": "MM.11","part": "a","q": "A point in 3D space has coordinates (3, −2, 5). How far is this point from the xy-plane?","a": "5 units","lvl": 1,"choices": ["3 units","2 units","5 units","6 units"],"steps": ["The xy-plane is where z = 0, so distance depends on z.","Take the absolute value of the z-coordinate in (3, −2, 5).","Match that distance to the choices and check your answer."]},{"id": "MM.11-02","std": "MM.11","part": "d","q": "What is the midpoint of the segment connecting (2, 4, 6) and (6, 8, 10)?","a": "(4, 6, 8)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["(8, 12, 16)","(4, 6, 8)","(2, 2, 2)","(3, 6, 8)"],"steps": ["Average the matching x-, y-, and z-coordinates.","Add each pair (2 + 6, 4 + 8, 6 + 10) and divide each sum by 2.","Write the ordered triple and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.11-03","std": "MM.11","part": "c","q": "What is the distance between the origin (0, 0, 0) and the point (3, 4, 0)?","a": "5 units","lvl": 1,"choices": ["25 units","12 units","5 units","7 units"],"steps": ["Square each coordinate difference, add them, and take the square root.","Set up d = √{3² + 4² + 0²}.","Add the squares, take the root, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.11-04","std": "MM.11","part": "c","q": "In a warehouse coordinate system (in meters), a drone hovers at (1, 2, 3) and a sensor is mounted at (4, 6, 3). What is the straight-line distance between them?","a": "5 meters","lvl": 2,"choices": ["5 meters","25 meters","6 meters","7 meters"],"steps": ["Find the change in x, y, and z between the two points.","Set up d = √{(4 − 1)² + (6 − 2)² + (3 − 3)²}.","Simplify inside the root, take the root, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.11-05","std": "MM.11","part": "d","q": "A support cable runs from anchor A at (−2, 5, 8) to anchor B at (6, −1, 4). Where should a central bracket be placed at the exact midpoint?","a": "(2, 2, 6)","lvl": 2,"choices": ["(2, 2, 6)","(4, 3, 2)","(2, 3, 6)","(4, 4, 12)"],"steps": ["Average the x-, y-, and z-values of both anchor points.","Add (−2 + 6), (5 + (−1)), and (8 + 4), then divide each by 2.","Form the midpoint triple and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.11-06","std": "MM.11","part": "b","q": "A right rectangular prism in Octant I has one vertex at the origin (0, 0, 0), edges along the positive x-, y-, and z-axes, and its opposite vertex at (4, 3, 5). What is the volume of the prism?","a": "60 cubic units","lvl": 2,"choices": ["60 cubic units","12 cubic units","94 cubic units","47 cubic units"],"steps": ["The opposite corner (4, 3, 5) gives the prism's length, width, height.","Set up V = 4 × 3 × 5.","Multiply the three edge lengths and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.11-07","std": "MM.11","part": "c","q": "A zip line starts at a platform at (2, −1, 4) and ends at a tower at (5, 3, 16), where coordinates are in meters. What is the length of the zip line?","a": "13 meters","lvl": 3,"choices": ["15 meters","169 meters","19 meters","13 meters"],"steps": ["Use the 3D distance formula on the x, y, and z differences.","Set up d = √{(5 − 2)² + (3 − (−1))² + (16 − 4)²}.","Square, add, take the root, and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.11-08","std": "MM.11","part": "d","q": "A structural beam has one endpoint at A(1, −3, 2) and its midpoint at M(4, 1, 5). What are the coordinates of the other endpoint B?","a": "(7, 5, 8)","lvl": 3,"choices": ["(2.5, −1, 3.5)","(7, 5, 8)","(5, −2, 7)","(7, −1, 8)"],"steps": ["To find the other endpoint, double each midpoint value and subtract A.","Compute 2(4) − 1 for x, 2(1) − (−3) for y, and 2(5) − 2 for z.","Check your triple by averaging it with A, then match the choices."]},{"id": "MM.11-09","std": "MM.11","part": "b","q": "P(−3, 4, 2) is a vertex of a box with one vertex at the origin and edges on the axes. Which axis vertices does the box use, and where is P?","a": "(−3, 0, 0), (0, 4, 0), (0, 0, 2); Octant II","lvl": 3,"choices": ["(3, 0, 0), (0, 4, 0), (0, 0, 2); Octant I","(0, −3, 0), (4, 0, 0), (0, 0, 2); Octant II","(−3, 0, 0), (0, 4, 0), (0, 0, 2); Octant II","(−3, 0, 0), (0, 4, 0), (0, 0, 2); Octant IV"],"steps": ["Start at (0, 0, 0) and move along each axis by the signed coordinates.","Name the three axis vertices, complete the box to P, and note signs.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.12-01","std": "MM.12","q": "A 3D-printed bracket corner at (−4, 2, 7) is translated 6 units in the positive x-direction, 5 units in the negative y-direction, and 3 units in the positive z-direction. What are its new coordinates?","a": "(2, −3, 10)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["(2, 7, 10)","(2, −3, 10)","(2, −3, 4)","(−10, 7, 4)"],"steps": ["Add each shift to the matching x-, y-, and z-coordinate.","Set up (−4 + 6, 2 − 5, 7 + 3).","Simplify each coordinate and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.12-02","std": "MM.12","q": "A drone waypoint at (−5, 8, −2) is reflected across the xz-plane. What are the coordinates of the reflected waypoint?","a": "(−5, −8, −2)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["(5, 8, −2)","(−5, −8, −2)","(5, −8, 2)","(−5, 8, 2)"],"steps": ["Reflecting across the xz-plane flips only the y-coordinate's sign.","Keep x = −5 and z = −2 the same, and change 8 to its opposite.","Write the new ordered triple and check it against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.12-03","std": "MM.12","q": "A sculpture vertex at (6, −9, 12) is dilated from the origin by a scale factor of 1/3. What are the new coordinates?","a": "(2, −3, 4)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["(2, 3, 4)","(3, −6, 9)","(18, −27, 36)","(2, −3, 4)"],"steps": ["Multiply every coordinate by the scale factor {1/3}.","Set up (6 × {1/3}, −9 × {1/3}, 12 × {1/3}).","Simplify each value and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.12-04","std": "MM.12","q": "A camera sensor at (0, 5, −3) is rotated 90° counterclockwise about the x-axis (viewed from the positive x-axis, sending (x, y, z) to (x, −z, y)). What are its new coordinates?","a": "(0, 3, 5)","lvl": 2,"choices": ["(0, −5, −3)","(5, 0, −3)","(0, −3, −5)","(0, 3, 5)"],"steps": ["Apply the given rotation rule (x, y, z) to (x, −z, y).","Plug in x = 0, y = 5, and z = −3 to get (0, −(−3), 5).","Simplify the signs carefully and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.12-05","std": "MM.12","q": "A furniture corner at (3, −2, 4) is dilated from the origin by a scale factor of 2 and then translated by (−1, 4, −5). What are the final coordinates?","a": "(5, 0, 3)","lvl": 2,"choices": ["(5, 0, 3)","(4, 4, −2)","(7, −8, 13)","(5, 2, 3)"],"steps": ["Do the transformations in order: dilate first, then translate.","Multiply (3, −2, 4) by 2, then add (−1, 4, −5) to the result.","Simplify all three coordinates and check against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.12-06","std": "MM.12","q": "A solar panel corner at (4, −1, 3) is rotated 180° about the y-axis. What are its new coordinates?","a": "(−4, −1, −3)","lvl": 2,"choices": ["(4, 1, −3)","(−4, −1, −3)","(−4, −1, 3)","(−4, 1, −3)"],"steps": ["A 180° turn about the y-axis keeps y and flips x and z.","Apply (x, y, z) to (−x, y, −z) on the point (4, −1, 3).","Write the new triple and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.12-07","std": "MM.12","q": "A point at (4, 2, −1) is rotated 270° counterclockwise about the z-axis (sending (x, y, z) to (y, −x, z)) and then reflected across the yz-plane. What are the resulting coordinates?","a": "(−2, −4, −1)","lvl": 3,"choices": ["(−2, 4, 1)","(−2, −4, −1)","(2, 4, −1)","(2, −4, −1)"],"steps": ["Apply the z-axis rotation first, then the yz-plane reflection.","Send (4, 2, −1) to (y, −x, z), then flip the new x-coordinate.","Verify each sign step by step and check against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.12-08","std": "MM.12","q": "A rectangular prism has a surface area of 54 sq cm. After a dilation from the origin with a scale factor of 2.5, what is the surface area of the enlarged prism?","a": "337.5 sq cm","lvl": 3,"choices": ["843.75 sq cm","135 sq cm","270 sq cm","337.5 sq cm"],"steps": ["Surface area scales by the square of the scale factor, k².","Set up 54 × (2.5)² to find the enlarged surface area.","Square 2.5 first, multiply by 54, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.12-09","std": "MM.12","q": "A game developer builds the left pillar of an archway (x < 0, centered on the z-axis). Which moves make the right pillar and then shift the archway 10 units along the positive y-axis?","a": "Reflect across the yz-plane, then translate every point by (0, 10, 0)","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Reflect across the xz-plane, then translate every point by (0, 10, 0)","Reflect across the yz-plane, then translate every point by (10, 0, 0)","Rotate 90° about the z-axis, then translate by (0, 0, 10)","Reflect across the yz-plane, then translate every point by (0, 10, 0)"],"steps": ["Choose the reflection plane that mirrors x < 0 across to x > 0.","Write the coordinate rule for that reflection and a +10 y-shift.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.13-01","std": "MM.13","q": "A natural history museum builds a 1:40 scale model of a 12-meter-long whale skeleton. How long is the model in centimeters?","a": "30 cm","lvl": 1,"choices": ["30 cm","48 cm","3 cm","300 cm"],"steps": ["Convert 12 meters to centimeters first (1 m = 100 cm).","Divide the centimeter length by 40: (12 × 100) ÷ 40.","Finish dividing and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.13-02","std": "MM.13","q": "A landscape architect's model uses the scale 1 inch = 15 feet. A pond measures 3.5 inches wide on the model. What is the actual width of the pond?","a": "52.5 feet","lvl": 1,"choices": ["60 feet","45 feet","52.5 feet","18.5 feet"],"steps": ["Multiply the model width in inches by 15 feet per inch.","Set up 3.5 × 15 to find the actual width in feet.","Finish multiplying and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.13-03","std": "MM.13","q": "An architectural model of a 90-foot water tower is 18 inches tall. What is the scale of the model?","a": "1 inch = 5 feet","lvl": 1,"choices": ["1 inch = 18 feet","1 inch = 6 feet","1 inch = 4 feet","1 inch = 5 feet"],"steps": ["Divide actual feet by model inches to find feet per 1 inch.","Set up 90 ÷ 18 to find how many feet equal 1 inch.","Write the scale as 1 inch = __ feet and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.13-04","std": "MM.13","q": "To model a barn whose peak cannot be measured directly, a student measures a 12-foot horizontal run from the outer wall to the center ridge and a 45° roof pitch above a 16-foot vertical wall. How tall is the barn on a 1 inch = 4 feet scale model?","a": "7 inches","lvl": 2,"choices": ["6 inches","4 inches","28 inches","7 inches"],"steps": ["Find the roof rise with trig, add the wall height, then scale.","Use rise = 12tan(45°), add 16 ft, then divide by 4 ft per in.","Finish the division and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.13-05","std": "MM.13","q": "A 1:5 scale prototype of an industrial fuel tank holds 4 liters of water. How many liters will the full-size tank hold?","a": "500 liters","lvl": 2,"choices": ["250 liters","500 liters","20 liters","100 liters"],"steps": ["Volume scales by the cube of the linear scale factor, k³.","Multiply the prototype volume by 5³: 4 × 5³.","Compute the cube first, multiply by 4, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.13-06","std": "MM.13","q": "A 1:12 scale dollhouse requires 3 square feet of paint to cover its exterior walls. How many square feet of paint are needed for the full-size house?","a": "432 sq ft","lvl": 2,"choices": ["432 sq ft","36 sq ft","5,184 sq ft","144 sq ft"],"steps": ["Area scales by the square of the linear scale factor, k².","Multiply the model paint area by 12²: 3 × 12².","Square 12 first, multiply by 3, and check against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.13-07","std": "MM.13","q": "A stadium dome is a hemisphere with a radius of 50 feet. A 1:100 scale 3D-printed model is created. What is the volume of the model dome in cubic inches, rounded to the nearest hundredth?","a": "452.39 in³","lvl": 3,"choices": ["113.10 in³","226.19 in³","452.39 in³","904.78 in³"],"steps": ["Scale the 50 ft radius by {1/100} and convert it to inches.","Use the hemisphere volume formula V = {2/3} × π × r³.","Compute, round to hundredths, and check against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.13-08","std": "MM.13","q": "On a 1:50 scale model of a courtyard centered at the origin, one corner lamppost is at (6, 4, 0) inches and a second is placed by reflecting it across the yz-plane to (−6, 4, 0) inches. What is the actual distance between these two lampposts in feet?","a": "50 feet","lvl": 3,"choices": ["50 feet","25 feet","600 feet","100 feet"],"steps": ["Find the model distance between (6, 4, 0) and (−6, 4, 0).","Multiply that distance in inches by 50, then divide by 12.","Simplify to feet and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.13-09","std": "MM.13","q": "A team builds a 1:100 scale model of a lighthouse (cylinder tower, cone roof, attached cottage). Which plan uses indirect measurement and the transformations correctly?","a": "Find the height with shadows or trig; dilate by 1/100; move pieces into place","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Measure the height with a tape measure; dilate by 100","Find the height with trig; dilate by 1/10; skip the other moves","Guess the height; translate the roof down onto the cottage","Find the height with shadows or trig; dilate by 1/100; move pieces into place"],"steps": ["Pick a hard-to-reach length, like height, using shadows or trig.","Connect dilation ({1/100}) to sizing and shifts and turns to assembly.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.14-01","std": "MM.14","q": "A cyclist rides at a steady speed of 14 miles per hour. How far does the cyclist travel in 3.5 hours?","a": "49 miles","lvl": 1,"choices": ["17.5 miles","49 miles","56 miles","42 miles"],"steps": ["Distance equals rate multiplied by time: d = r × t.","Set up d = 14 × 3.5.","Multiply carefully and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.14-02","std": "MM.14","q": "A video starts with 500 views and triples every day. How many views does it have after 2 days?","a": "4,500 views","lvl": 1,"choices": ["1,500 views","4,500 views","13,500 views","3,000 views"],"steps": ["Tripling each day means multiplying by 3 for each day.","Set up 500 × 3² for 2 days of growth.","Evaluate the power first, multiply, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.14-03","std": "MM.14","q": "A pendulum completes one full back-and-forth swing every 4 seconds. How many full cycles does it complete in 1 minute?","a": "15 cycles","lvl": 1,"choices": ["24 cycles","12 cycles","15 cycles","240 cycles"],"steps": ["Convert 1 minute to 60 seconds so the time units match.","Divide total seconds by seconds per cycle: 60 ÷ 4.","Finish dividing and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.14-04","std": "MM.14","q": "After water treatment, a pond loses 10% of its algae mass each week. If the pond starts with 800 kg of algae, how much algae remains after 3 weeks?","a": "583.2 kg","lvl": 2,"choices": ["648.0 kg","240.0 kg","560.0 kg","583.2 kg"],"steps": ["Losing 10% leaves 90%, so the weekly decay factor is 0.90.","Set up A = 800 × (0.90)³.","Evaluate the power, multiply by 800, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.14-05","std": "MM.14","q": "A drone's altitude in feet is modeled by h(t) = −4t² + 24t + 10, where t is time in seconds. What is the drone's maximum altitude?","a": "46 feet","lvl": 2,"choices": ["34 feet","82 feet","46 feet","58 feet"],"steps": ["The maximum of a downward parabola occurs at its vertex.","Find t = {−b/2a} = {−24/(2 × −4)}, then plug t into h(t).","Evaluate h(t) carefully and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.14-06","std": "MM.14","q": "Water depth at a coastal pier cycles sinusoidally between a low tide of 4 feet and a high tide of 14 feet. What are the midline and amplitude of the depth model?","a": "Midline 9 feet, amplitude 5 feet","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Midline 10 feet, amplitude 5 feet","Midline 7 feet, amplitude 4 feet","Midline 9 feet, amplitude 10 feet","Midline 9 feet, amplitude 5 feet"],"steps": ["Midline is the average of max and min; amplitude is half the gap.","Compute {(14 + 4)/2} for midline and {(14 − 4)/2} for amplitude.","Pair both results and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.14-07","std": "MM.14","q": "A patient takes a 400 mg dose of medication that decreases by 25% every 6 hours. How much medication remains in the bloodstream after 18 hours?","a": "168.75 mg","lvl": 3,"choices": ["100.00 mg","168.75 mg","126.56 mg","225.00 mg"],"steps": ["Find how many 6-hour periods fit in 18 hours and the decay factor.","Set up 400 × (1 − 0.25)^{n} using n = 18 ÷ 6.","Evaluate carefully and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.14-08","std": "MM.14","q": "A wind turbine blade tip reaches a maximum height of 95 meters and a minimum height of 25 meters, completing one full rotation every 6 seconds. If the blade tip starts at its maximum height at t = 0, which function models its height h(t)?","a": "h(t) = 35cos((π/3)t) + 60","lvl": 3,"choices": ["h(t) = 35cos((π/3)t) + 60","h(t) = 35cos(6t) + 60","h(t) = 35sin((π/3)t) + 60","h(t) = 70cos((π/3)t) + 60"],"steps": ["Starting at a maximum points to a positive cosine model.","Find amp {(95 − 25)/2}, mid {(95 + 25)/2}, and b = {2π/6}.","Put A, b, and d into A×cos(bt) + d and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.14-09","std": "MM.14","q": "A rabbit population on an island was modeled by P(t) = 50(1.3)^t, but after 10 years the actual population is far lower. Why, and how should the model be revised?","a": "It ignored carrying capacity; switch to a logistic model that levels off","lvl": 3,"choices": ["The model is fine; the data must be wrong","Rabbit populations always decrease; use a decay model","The growth factor was too small; use 1.5 instead","It ignored carrying capacity; switch to a logistic model that levels off"],"steps": ["Spot the hidden assumption in an exponential growth model.","Link limited food and space to carrying capacity and logistic growth.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.15-01","std": "MM.15","part": "b","q": "A scatter plot shows that as average outdoor temperature increases, a home's monthly heating bill decreases steadily along a straight line. How is this relationship described?","a": "Negative linear association","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Exponential growth","Positive linear association","Negative linear association","No association"],"steps": ["Check the shape (straight line) and direction (downward).","One value rising as the other falls means a negative trend.","Combine shape and direction, then check against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.15-02","std": "MM.15","part": "c","q": "A linear regression equation for a maple tree's height y (in feet) after x years is y = 1.6x + 3. What is the predicted height after 10 years?","a": "19 feet","lvl": 1,"choices": ["31.6 feet","13 feet","16 feet","19 feet"],"steps": ["Substitute x = 10 into the regression equation.","Set up y = 1.6 × 10 + 3.","Multiply first, then add, and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.15-03","std": "MM.15","part": "a","q": "When creating a scatter plot to model how a car's age affects its resale price, which variable belongs on the horizontal (x) axis?","a": "Car age","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Resale price","Car color","Either variable, since both are responses","Car age"],"steps": ["The explanatory (independent) variable goes on the x-axis.","Ask which variable predicts or explains the other.","Match the explanatory variable to the choices and check."]},{"id": "MM.15-04","std": "MM.15","part": "b","q": "A scatter plot of a mug of hot coffee's temperature over time drops rapidly at first and then levels off near room temperature (decreasing and concave up). Which regression model best fits this pattern?","a": "Exponential decay","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Sinusoidal","Linear","Exponential decay","Exponential growth"],"steps": ["Look at how the rate of decrease changes over time.","Falling fast then flattening toward a floor fits an asymptote.","Match that curve behavior to the choices and check your pick."]},{"id": "MM.15-05","std": "MM.15","part": "c","q": "Graphing technology yields the exponential regression y = 120 × (1.25)^x for a club's newsletter subscribers y after x weeks. What is the predicted number of subscribers at week 3, rounded to the nearest whole number?","a": "234 subscribers","lvl": 2,"choices": ["234 subscribers","450 subscribers","210 subscribers","188 subscribers"],"steps": ["Plug x = 3 into the exponential regression model.","Set up y = 120 × (1.25)³.","Cube 1.25 first, multiply, round, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.15-06","std": "MM.15","part": "b","q": "A quadratic regression of monthly profit (y) versus ticket price (x) rises to a single peak at (45, 18000) and then falls. What does (45, 18000) represent?","a": "A ticket price of §45 gives the maximum monthly profit of §18,000","lvl": 2,"choices": ["A ticket price of §45 gives the maximum monthly profit of §18,000","The company must sell 18,000 tickets to break even","Profit increases by §45 for every §18,000 in sales","At §45 per ticket, the profit drops to zero"],"steps": ["Match each coordinate to its axis: x is price, y is profit.","Read a single peak as the maximum y-value at that x-value.","Interpret (45, 18000) in context and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.15-07","std": "MM.15","part": "c","q": "A quadratic regression for a car's stopping distance y (in feet) at speed x (in mph) is y = 0.05x² + 1.2x. How much longer is the predicted stopping distance at 60 mph than at 40 mph?","a": "124 feet","lvl": 3,"choices": ["124 feet","100 feet","24 feet","252 feet"],"steps": ["Evaluate the model at both speeds, then find the difference.","Compute y(60) and y(40) using 0.05x² + 1.2x, then subtract.","Finish the subtraction and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.15-08","std": "MM.15","part": "b","q": "A scatter plot of smartphone adoption over 15 years is S-shaped: concave up from year 0 to year 6, and concave down from year 6 to year 15 as it approaches 95%. What is the feature at year 6?","a": "An inflection point where the rate of adoption is fastest","lvl": 3,"choices": ["A horizontal asymptote","An inflection point where the rate of adoption is fastest","A local maximum where adoption stops","A local minimum"],"steps": ["Recall the name for a point where concavity switches.","On an S-curve, the concavity switch marks the steepest growth.","Match that feature and meaning to the choices and check."]},{"id": "MM.15-09","std": "MM.15","part": "a","q": "A student models a year of electricity use with only the January through April bills (r² = 0.98). Why is this not enough data?","a": "It misses summer peaks; collect 12 to 24 straight months","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Electricity use does not change during the year","Four points are too many for a linear model","r² = 0.98 proves the model fits the whole year","It misses summer peaks; collect 12 to 24 straight months"],"steps": ["Think about how electricity use changes across all four seasons.","Note what Jan–Apr misses and how many months show a full cycle.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.16-01","std": "MM.16","q": "In a linearized model using common logarithms, log(y) = 3. What is the value of y?","a": "1,000","lvl": 1,"choices": ["3","1,000","30","100"],"steps": ["A common log uses base 10, so log(y) = 3 rewrites as a power.","Convert the log equation to y = 10³.","Evaluate the power of 10 and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.16-02","std": "MM.16","q": "Which coordinate transformation linearizes a power function of the form y = a × x^b?","a": "Plot log(x) versus log(y)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Plot x² versus y","Plot log(x) versus y","Plot x versus log(y)","Plot log(x) versus log(y)"],"steps": ["Take log of both sides of y = a × x^{b} to see what becomes linear.","Rewrite as log(y) = b × log(x) + log(a) to spot the new axes.","Match those two transformed variables to the choices and check."]},{"id": "MM.16-03","std": "MM.16","q": "In a model linearized with natural logarithms, ln(y) = 0. What is the value of y?","a": "1","lvl": 1,"choices": ["2.72","10","0","1"],"steps": ["The natural log ln(y) has base e.","Rewrite ln(y) = 0 in exponential form: y = e⁰.","Use the zero-exponent rule and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.16-04","std": "MM.16","q": "A linearized exponential dataset produces the equation log(y) = 0.25x + 1.5. What is the predicted value of y when x = 6?","a": "1,000","lvl": 2,"choices": ["1,000","300","3","100"],"steps": ["Plug x = 6 into the linear equation to find log(y) first.","Compute 0.25 × 6 + 1.5, then rewrite log(y) as y = 10^{value}.","Evaluate the power of 10 and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.16-05","std": "MM.16","q": "A lab experiment linearized with natural logarithms gives ln(y) = 0.5x + 2. What is the predicted value of y when x = 4, rounded to the nearest hundredth?","a": "54.60","lvl": 2,"choices": ["10,000.00","20.09","54.60","4.00"],"steps": ["Substitute x = 4 to find ln(y), then convert using base e.","Compute 0.5 × 4 + 2, then set up y = e^{value}.","Evaluate with a calculator, round, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.16-06","std": "MM.16","q": "Linearizing a power dataset gives log(y) = 3log(x) + 0.699. Given that 10^{0.699} is approximately 5, rewrite this relationship in nonlinear form.","a": "y = 5 × x³","lvl": 2,"choices": ["y = 5 × 3^x","y = 5 × x³","y = 0.699 × x³","y = 3 × x⁵"],"steps": ["Match log(y) = b × log(x) + log(a) to a power model y = a × x^{b}.","Use 10^{0.699} for a and the slope 3 for the exponent b.","Write y = a × x^{b} and check your equation against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.16-07","std": "MM.16","q": "A scatter plot of x versus log(y) forms a straight line passing through (0, 2) and (4, 3). What is the original exponential equation y = a × b^x, with b rounded to the nearest hundredth?","a": "y = 100 × (1.78)^x","lvl": 3,"choices": ["y = 2 × (0.25)^x","y = 100 × (1.78)^x","y = 20 × (1.78)^x","y = 100 × (0.25)^x"],"steps": ["Find the line's slope and y-intercept for log(y) = mx + c.","Convert back with a = 10^{c} and b = 10^{m} using m = {(3 − 2)/(4 − 0)}.","Round b to hundredths and check y = a × b^{x} in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.16-08","std": "MM.16","q": "A physicist plots x versus log(y) and sees a clear curve in the residuals, but plotting log(x) versus log(y) gives a straight line with slope 1.5 and random residuals. What type of function best models the original data?","a": "A power function of the form y = a × x^{1.5}","lvl": 3,"choices": ["An exponential function of the form y = a × (1.5)^x","A linear function with slope 1.5","A power function of the form y = a × x^{1.5}","A logarithmic function y = 1.5log(x)"],"steps": ["Identify which transformation made the plot straight.","A straight log(x) vs log(y) plot means y = a × x^{b} with slope b.","Insert the given slope as the exponent and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.16-09","std": "MM.16","q": "Radioactive decay data linearizes to log(y) = −0.301t + 2.602 (t in hours). With 10^{−0.301} ≈ 0.5 and 10^{2.602} ≈ 400, what is the exponential model?","a": "y = 400(0.5)^t: 400 g at the start, half left each hour","lvl": 3,"choices": ["y = 400(0.5)^t: 400 g at the start, half left each hour","y = 400(0.301)^t: 400 g, losing 30.1% each hour","y = 0.5(400)^t: 0.5 g, growing each hour","y = 2.602(0.301)^t: 2.6 g at the start"],"steps": ["Use base 10 on both sides to rewrite log(y) = mt + c as a × b^{t}.","Substitute the given powers of 10 for initial mass a and factor b.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.17-01","std": "MM.17","q": "Which of the following is NOT a statistical question?","a": "What is the current population of Birmingham, Alabama?","lvl": 1,"choices": ["What percentage of Alabama drivers wear seatbelts?","How many miles do teachers at our school commute each day?","How much do 10th graders spend on lunch each week?","What is the current population of Birmingham, Alabama?"],"steps": ["A statistical question expects variability across many data values.","Look for the question with one fixed factual answer.","Pick the non-statistical question and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.17-02","std": "MM.17","q": "In the Statistical Problem-Solving Cycle, what is the final step after analyzing the collected data?","a": "Interpret the results in the context of the original question","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Remove any data points that disagree with the hypothesis","Formulate a statistical question","Collect a second dataset","Interpret the results in the context of the original question"],"steps": ["Think about what comes after collecting and analyzing data.","After calculations finish, connect findings back to the question.","Match that final phase to the choices and check your answer."]},{"id": "MM.17-03","std": "MM.17","q": "A researcher records whether students who choose to eat breakfast earn higher math grades, without assigning anyone what to eat. What type of study is this?","a": "An observational study","lvl": 1,"choices": ["An observational study","A randomized experiment","A simulation","A double-blind clinical trial"],"steps": ["Check whether the researcher assigned treatments or just watched.","Recording choices people already made means no treatment imposed.","Match that study type to the choices and check your answer."]},{"id": "MM.17-04","std": "MM.17","q": "A principal wants to estimate the average daily screen time of all 1,200 students at her school. She randomly selects 80 students from the school roster to survey. What are the population and the sample?","a": "Population: all 1,200 students; Sample: the 80 selected students","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Population: the 80 selected students; Sample: all 1,200 students","Population: daily screen time; Sample: 80 hours","Population: all 1,200 students; Sample: the 80 selected students","Population: all high schoolers in the state; Sample: all 1,200 students"],"steps": ["Population is the full group; sample is the smaller group surveyed.","Match the full school roster to population and the 80 to sample.","Confirm both labels in order and check against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.17-05","std": "MM.17","q": "A bakery tests whether a new yeast makes bread rise higher by randomly assigning 20 dough batches to the new yeast and 20 to standard yeast, baking all loaves in the same oven at 375°F. Why is using the same oven temperature important?","a": "It controls a potential confounding variable","lvl": 2,"choices": ["It increases the margin of error","It eliminates the need for a control group","It creates a simple random sample","It controls a potential confounding variable"],"steps": ["Ask what could happen if one yeast group baked in a hotter oven.","Keeping temperature identical isolates the effect of the yeast.","Match that design purpose to the choices and check your pick."]},{"id": "MM.17-06","std": "MM.17","q": "A study randomly assigns 100 office volunteers to use standing desks or sitting desks for 3 months and finds significantly less back pain in the standing desk group. What conclusion is justified?","a": "Standing desks caused lower back pain for these 100 office volunteers","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Standing desks caused lower back pain for these 100 office volunteers","Only correlation, not causation, can be concluded","Standing desks cure back pain for every person in the world","The results are invalid because volunteers were used"],"steps": ["Check if the study used random assignment and random sampling.","Random assignment tests cause; random sampling allows generalizing.","Match what this study can claim to the choices and check."]},{"id": "MM.17-07","std": "MM.17","q": "A health study tracks 5,000 people who already drink green tea daily and 5,000 who do not, finding lower heart disease rates among tea drinkers. Why can researchers NOT conclude that green tea prevents heart disease?","a": "It is an observational study; tea drinkers may also exercise more or eat healthier (confounding variables)","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Heart disease cannot be measured as a categorical variable","It is an observational study; tea drinkers may also exercise more or eat healthier (confounding variables)","Observational studies can never identify associations","The sample size of 10,000 is too small"],"steps": ["Decide if people chose to drink tea or were randomly assigned.","Without random assignment, other health habits can explain results.","Match that reasoning to the choices and check your answer."]},{"id": "MM.17-08","std": "MM.17","q": "A school district wants to estimate parent approval of a new dress code across 5 schools of different sizes. Why is a stratified random sample by school better than surveying parents who attend a PTA meeting?","a": "A stratified random sample represents all 5 schools without voluntary response bias, whereas PTA attendees are a biased convenience sample","lvl": 3,"choices": ["A stratified random sample guarantees 100% approval","A stratified random sample represents all 5 schools without voluntary response bias, whereas PTA attendees are a biased convenience sample","Stratified sampling turns the survey into a randomized experiment","PTA meetings have too many parents to count"],"steps": ["Compare who shows up to a PTA meeting versus all district parents.","Stratifying by school ensures every school is fairly represented.","Match that bias comparison to the choices and check your pick."]},{"id": "MM.17-09","std": "MM.17","q": "Which plan uses all four phases of the Statistical Problem-Solving Cycle to learn whether adults support Sunday library hours?","a": "Ask a question, survey a random sample, find the proportion, interpret","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Ask a question, survey a random sample, find the proportion, interpret","Survey 400 adults at the library on Sunday","Post a library poll and report the votes","Ask the library staff and report their opinion"],"steps": ["List the four phases: formulate, collect, analyze, and interpret.","Plan a random sample, neutral question, and proportion estimate.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.18-01","std": "MM.18","part": "a","q": "A weather station records measurable rain on 18 out of 60 spring days. What is the empirical probability of rain on a spring day?","a": "0.30","lvl": 1,"choices": ["0.18","0.30","0.60","0.42"],"steps": ["Empirical probability is observed successes divided by total trials.","Set up {18/60} and convert the fraction to a decimal.","Finish dividing and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.18-02","std": "MM.18","part": "b","q": "In a cumulative relative frequency graph (ogive), what height does the curve always reach at the largest data value?","a": "1","lvl": 1,"choices": ["0","1","0.5","The mean of the distribution"],"steps": ["Cumulative relative frequency adds proportions up to each value.","At the largest value, 100% of the data has been included.","Write 100% as a proportion and check against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.18-03","std": "MM.18","part": "c","q": "Which measures of center and spread are most appropriate for a roughly symmetric, bell-shaped probability distribution with no outliers?","a": "Mean and standard deviation","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Mode and range","Mean and standard deviation","Median and interquartile range (IQR)","Median and range"],"steps": ["Match center and spread measures to the distribution's shape.","When there are no outliers or skew, non-resistant measures work best.","Pick the pair best for symmetric data and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.18-04","std": "MM.18","part": "c","q": "An empirical probability distribution for the number of cars waiting at a coffee drive-thru is: 0 cars (0.1), 1 car (0.3), 2 cars (0.4), 3 cars (0.2). What is the mean number of cars waiting?","a": "1.7 cars","lvl": 2,"choices": ["1.5 cars","1.7 cars","2.0 cars","1.2 cars"],"steps": ["The mean of a discrete distribution sums each value × probability.","Set up 0(0.1) + 1(0.3) + 2(0.4) + 3(0.2).","Multiply each pair, add them up, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.18-05","std": "MM.18","part": "b","q": "The relative frequencies for a class's quiz scores are: 70 (0.10), 80 (0.25), 90 (0.45), and 100 (0.20). What is the cumulative relative frequency at a score of 80?","a": "0.35","lvl": 2,"choices": ["0.25","0.35","0.65","0.80"],"steps": ["Cumulative relative frequency adds all frequencies up to 80.","Add the relative frequencies for 70 and 80: 0.10 + 0.25.","Finish adding and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.18-06","std": "MM.18","part": "a","q": "A sales simulation runs 250 customer calls and 45 result in a purchase. Based on this empirical probability, how many purchases are expected in 1,000 calls?","a": "180 purchases","lvl": 2,"choices": ["180 purchases","125 purchases","45 purchases","225 purchases"],"steps": ["Find the empirical probability, then multiply by 1,000 calls.","Set up {45/250} × 1,000.","Simplify the product and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.18-07","std": "MM.18","part": "c","q": "A discrete probability distribution has values X = 0 (p = 0.3), X = 2 (p = 0.4), and X = 4 (p = 0.3), with mean 2. What is the standard deviation of X, rounded to the nearest hundredth?","a": "1.55","lvl": 3,"choices": ["1.20","1.55","2.00","2.40"],"steps": ["Find variance by summing (X − mean)² × p, then take its root.","Compute √{(0 − 2)²(0.3) + (2 − 2)²(0.4) + (4 − 2)²(0.3)}.","Take the square root, round to hundredths, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.18-08","std": "MM.18","part": "c","q": "A charity raffle sells tickets for §5 each. The payout probabilities per ticket are: §0 (0.85), §10 (0.10), and §50 (0.05). What is a player's expected net gain or loss per ticket?","a": "Expected loss of §1.50","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Expected gain of §1.50","Expected loss of §1.50","Expected gain of §3.50","Expected loss of §3.50"],"steps": ["Find the expected payout first, then subtract the §5 ticket cost.","Compute 0(0.85) + 10(0.10) + 50(0.05), then subtract 5.","Interpret the sign as gain or loss and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.18-09","std": "MM.18","part": "b","q": "Over 100 days a website had 0 outages on 72 days, 1 on 18, 2 on 7 and 3 on 3. Which describes the distribution?","a": "Right-skewed, mean 0.41; the median of 0 better shows a typical day","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Right-skewed, mean 1; the median of 1 shows a typical day","Symmetric, mean 1.5; the mean shows a typical day","Right-skewed, mean 0.41; the median of 0 better shows a typical day","Left-skewed, mean 0.41; the mean shows a typical day"],"steps": ["Check where the peak sits and which direction the tail stretches.","Compute the mean with sum of value × proportion, and find median.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.19-01","std": "MM.19","part": "a","q": "A multiple-choice question has 4 options, so guessing gives a 0.25 probability of being correct. If a student guesses on 2 independent questions, what is the probability of getting both right?","a": "0.0625","lvl": 1,"choices": ["0.0625","0.125","0.50","0.25"],"steps": ["For independent events, multiply their probabilities together.","Set up 0.25 × 0.25.","Multiply carefully and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.19-02","std": "MM.19","part": "c","q": "A population of battery lifetimes has a mean of 40 hours and a standard deviation of 8 hours. For random samples of size n = 16, what is the standard deviation of the sample mean?","a": "2 hours","lvl": 1,"choices": ["0.5 hours","4 hours","2 hours","8 hours"],"steps": ["The standard deviation of the sample mean is σ ÷ √{n}.","Plug in σ = 8 and n = 16 to get 8 ÷ √{16}.","Take the root, divide, and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.19-03","std": "MM.19","part": "b","q": "Can a normal approximation be used for the sampling distribution of a sample proportion when n = 40 and p = 0.10?","a": "No, because np = 4 is less than 10","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Yes, because n ≥ 30","No, because np = 4 is less than 10","No, because p is not 0.50","Yes, because n(1 − p) = 36 is at least 10"],"steps": ["Normal approximation requires both np ≥ 10 and n(1 − p) ≥ 10.","Compute 40 × 0.10 and 40 × 0.90 to test both conditions.","See if both reach 10, then check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.19-04","std": "MM.19","part": "a","q": "A packet of flower seeds has a 70% germination rate. If 3 seeds are planted independently, what is the probability that exactly 2 seeds germinate?","a": "0.441","lvl": 2,"choices": ["0.441","0.490","0.343","0.147"],"steps": ["Use the binomial rule: (ways to pick 2 of 3) × p² × (1 − p)¹.","There are 3 orders, so set up 3 × (0.70)² × (0.30).","Evaluate the product and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.19-05","std": "MM.19","part": "b","q": "In a large city, 36% of households own a dog (p = 0.36). For random samples of size n = 64, what is the standard deviation of the sample proportion?","a": "0.06","lvl": 2,"choices": ["0.0036","0.06","0.045","0.48"],"steps": ["Use the sample proportion SD formula: √{p(1 − p) ÷ n}.","Plug in p = 0.36 and n = 64: √{0.36 × 0.64 ÷ 64}.","Simplify inside the root, take the root, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.19-06","std": "MM.19","part": "d","q": "The sampling distribution of a sample mean is approximately normal with mean 120 and standard deviation 5. Using the Empirical Rule, what is the probability a random sample mean falls between 110 and 130?","a": "95%","lvl": 2,"choices": ["99.7%","50%","95%","68%"],"steps": ["Find how many standard deviations 110 and 130 are from 120.","Compute {(130 − 120)/5} and recall the 68-95-99.7 rule.","Match that number of SDs to its percent and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.19-07","std": "MM.19","part": "c","q": "A right-skewed population of package weights has mean 50 lb and standard deviation 15 lb. For random samples of size n = 225, what is the approximate probability that a sample mean is less than 49 lb?","a": "16% (or 0.159)","lvl": 3,"choices": ["50% (or 0.500)","34% (or 0.341)","16% (or 0.159)","2.5% (or 0.023)"],"steps": ["With n = 225, CLT gives a normal sample mean with SD = 15 ÷ √{225}.","Find how many SDs below 50 the value 49 sits, then find the tail.","Use the 68% middle rule to find the lower tail and check choices."]},{"id": "MM.19-08","std": "MM.19","part": "d","q": "A fair six-sided die is rolled 5 times. What is the probability of rolling at least one 6, rounded to the nearest thousandth?","a": "0.598","lvl": 3,"choices": ["0.833","0.167","0.402","0.598"],"steps": ["Use the complement: 1 minus the chance of rolling zero 6s.","Set up 1 − ({5/6})⁵.","Evaluate, round to thousandths, and check against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.19-09","std": "MM.19","part": "b","q": "Samples of n = 200 chips come from a line with a 10% defect rate. What range of sample proportions is expected about 95% of the time?","a": "About 0.058 to 0.142 (mean 0.10, SD about 0.021)","lvl": 3,"choices": ["About 0.04 to 0.16 (mean 0.10, SD 0.03)","About 0.079 to 0.121 (mean 0.10, SD about 0.021)","About 0.10 to 0.20 (mean 0.15, SD 0.025)","About 0.058 to 0.142 (mean 0.10, SD about 0.021)"],"steps": ["Verify np ≥ 10 and n(1 − p) ≥ 10 using n = 200 and p = 0.10.","Find mean = p and SD = √{p(1 − p) ÷ n}, then form mean ± 2 × SD.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.20-01","std": "MM.20","part": "a","q": "A commuter survey estimates an average commute time of 26 minutes with a margin of error of 2.5 minutes. What is the confidence interval?","a": "(23.5, 28.5) minutes","lvl": 1,"choices": ["(23.5, 26.0) minutes","(23.5, 28.5) minutes","(26.0, 28.5) minutes","(21.0, 31.0) minutes"],"steps": ["A confidence interval is estimate minus ME to estimate plus ME.","Set up 26 − 2.5 for the lower bound and 26 + 2.5 for the upper.","Compute both endpoints and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.20-02","std": "MM.20","part": "c","q": "A cereal box is labeled 16 ounces. A quality inspector tests whether the filling machine is off-target (either overfilling or underfilling). What are the null and alternative hypotheses?","a": "H0: μ = 16, Ha: μ ≠ 16","lvl": 1,"choices": ["H0: μ = 16, Ha: μ > 16","H0: μ ≠ 16, Ha: μ = 16","H0: μ = 16, Ha: μ ≠ 16","H0: μ = 16, Ha: μ < 16"],"steps": ["The null hypothesis H0 always states equality (no change).","Testing over OR underfilling calls for a two-sided ≠ in Ha.","Match both hypotheses to the choices and check your pick."]},{"id": "MM.20-03","std": "MM.20","part": "d","q": "A significance test yields a p-value of 0.14 at a significance level of α = 0.05. What is the correct decision?","a": "Fail to reject the null hypothesis","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Prove the null hypothesis is 100% true","Fail to reject the null hypothesis","Lower α to 0.01","Reject the null hypothesis"],"steps": ["Compare the p-value to α: reject H0 only if p-value ≤ α.","Check whether 0.14 is less than or greater than 0.05.","Make the matching decision and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.20-04","std": "MM.20","part": "a","q": "In a random sample of 100 customers, 60% prefer online billing (p̂ = 0.60, standard error = 0.049). Using a critical value of 1.96, what is the 95% confidence interval rounded to three decimal places?","a": "(0.504, 0.696)","lvl": 2,"choices": ["(0.504, 0.696)","(0.502, 0.698)","(0.551, 0.649)","(0.404, 0.796)"],"steps": ["Multiply the critical value by SE to get the margin of error.","Compute ME = 1.96 × 0.049, then form 0.60 − ME and 0.60 + ME.","Round both bounds to three decimals and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.20-05","std": "MM.20","part": "b","q": "A 95% confidence interval for the mean wait time at an urgent care clinic is (14.2, 18.8) minutes. Which interpretation is correct?","a": "We are 95% confident that the true population mean wait time is between 14.2 and 18.8 minutes","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Exactly 95% of clinics have wait times in this interval","We are 95% confident that the true population mean wait time is between 14.2 and 18.8 minutes","95% of all patients wait between 14.2 and 18.8 minutes","There is a 95% chance the sample mean is between 14.2 and 18.8 minutes"],"steps": ["A confidence interval estimates the true population parameter.","Eliminate choices about individual patients or the sample mean.","Pick the proper population-mean statement and check choices."]},{"id": "MM.20-06","std": "MM.20","part": "d","q": "In a hypothesis test, what is a Type II error?","a": "Failing to reject a false null hypothesis","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Rejecting a true null hypothesis","Rejecting a false null hypothesis","Failing to reject a false null hypothesis","Failing to reject a true null hypothesis"],"steps": ["Recall the two decision errors: rejecting true H0 or missing false H0.","Match Type I to a false positive and Type II to a false negative.","Connect Type II to the decision on H0 and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.20-07","std": "MM.20","part": "c","q": "A manufacturer claims its steel bolts have a mean length of 50 mm. A random sample of 36 bolts has a mean of 49.4 mm and a standard deviation of 1.2 mm. What is the t-test statistic for H0: μ = 50?","a": "−3.0","lvl": 3,"choices": ["−18.0","3.0","−0.5","−3.0"],"steps": ["Use the t-statistic formula: t = (x̄ − μ) ÷ (s ÷ √{n}).","Find SE = 1.2 ÷ √{36}, then divide (49.4 − 50) by that SE.","Keep the negative sign, simplify, and check against choices."]},{"id": "MM.20-08","std": "MM.20","part": "b","q": "A city transit authority will fund a new bus route only if more than 40% of commuters would use it. A random survey produces a 95% confidence interval of (43%, 51%). What strategic decision is supported?","a": "Fund the bus route, because the entire confidence interval lies above 40%","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Fund the bus route, because the entire confidence interval lies above 40%","Do not fund the route, because 51% is barely above half","Wait until the confidence interval includes 40%","No decision can be made because 40% is not inside the interval"],"steps": ["Compare the entire confidence interval to the 40% threshold.","Check whether every plausible value from 43% to 51% beats 40%.","Match that evidence to the decision and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.20-09","std": "MM.20","part": "d","q": "A brake maker tests H0: brakes meet safety standards against Ha: brakes are defective. Which error is more dangerous, and how should α be set?","a": "Type II (missing bad brakes); use a larger α such as 0.10","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Type II (missing bad brakes); use a larger α such as 0.10","Type II (missing bad brakes); use a smaller α such as 0.01","Neither; α should always be 0.05","Type I (recalling good brakes); use a smaller α such as 0.01"],"steps": ["Define Type I (false alarm) and Type II (missed defect) in context.","Weigh safety vs recall cost, then note how larger α cuts Type II.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.21-01","std": "MM.21","q": "A restaurant leaves optional comment cards on tables and reports that 90% of diners are unhappy with service. What type of bias most likely distorts this result?","a": "Voluntary response bias","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Placebo effect","Random sampling error","Cluster bias","Voluntary response bias"],"steps": ["Notice that diners chose on their own whether to respond.","Optional feedback mostly draws people with strong complaints.","Match that self-selection bias to the choices and check."]},{"id": "MM.21-02","std": "MM.21","q": "A polling firm calls only landline phone numbers to estimate the voting preferences of young adults aged 18 to 25. What type of bias occurs?","a": "Undercoverage bias","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Random error","Undercoverage bias","Wording bias","Response bias"],"steps": ["Ask which part of the 18–25 population is left out by landlines.","Excluding a large group from the sampling frame causes bias.","Pick the bias name for leaving groups out and check choices."]},{"id": "MM.21-03","std": "MM.21","q": "If an online website increases its biased voluntary-response poll from 500 clicks to 50,000 clicks, what happens to the bias?","a": "The bias remains because larger sample sizes do not fix a biased sampling method","lvl": 1,"choices": ["The bias is completely eliminated","The bias is reduced by a factor of 10","The poll becomes a simple random sample","The bias remains because larger sample sizes do not fix a biased sampling method"],"steps": ["Separate sample size (precision) from sampling method (bias).","More clicks shrink random error, not a flawed selection method.","Decide what happens to the bias and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.21-04","std": "MM.21","q": "A school administrator stands in the hallway and asks students face-to-face if they have ever cheated on an exam. What bias will likely cause the cheating rate to be underestimated?","a": "Response bias (social desirability bias)","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Sampling variability","Nonresponse bias","Undercoverage bias","Response bias (social desirability bias)"],"steps": ["Think about how students answer a sensitive question face-to-face.","People often hide socially frowned-upon behavior from authority.","Match that inaccurate-answering bias to the choices and check."]},{"id": "MM.21-05","std": "MM.21","q": "A blog claims sparkling water boosts mood based on a 12-person study that found a slight increase with p = 0.18. What is the strongest statistical critique?","a": "The p-value of 0.18 is not statistically significant, so the observed difference could easily be due to random chance","lvl": 2,"choices": ["A p-value must be above 0.50 to show an effect","The p-value of 0.18 is not statistically significant, so the observed difference could easily be due to random chance","Mood cannot be studied in statistics","12 people is always enough to prove causation"],"steps": ["Compare p = 0.18 to a standard significance level like 0.05.","A large p-value means the result could easily happen by chance.","Pick the critique about significance and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.21-06","std": "MM.21","q": "A toothpaste ad claims '4 out of 5 dentists recommend Brand Z' without stating how many dentists were surveyed or whether they could recommend multiple brands. Why should a consumer be skeptical?","a": "Missing details on sample size, sampling method, and question wording make the claim unverifiable and potentially misleading","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Missing details on sample size, sampling method, and question wording make the claim unverifiable and potentially misleading","Because 4 out of 5 is less than 50%","Because dentists cannot be surveyed statistically","Because 5 dentists is the entire population"],"steps": ["List what a trustworthy survey report must disclose.","Look at what the ad leaves out: sample size, method, and wording.","Pick the critique about missing details and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.21-07","std": "MM.21","q": "A poll reports Candidate A leads Candidate B by 51% to 49% with a margin of error of ±3%. A headline declares, 'Candidate A Clearly Ahead!' Why is the headline statistically flawed?","a": "The 2-percentage-point difference is well within the ±3% margin of error, making the race a statistical tie","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Candidate A needs at least 75% to be ahead","Polls cannot be used for elections","The 2-percentage-point difference is well within the ±3% margin of error, making the race a statistical tie","A margin of error of ±3% is too large to publish"],"steps": ["Compare the 2% lead to the ±3% margin of error.","Check whether the two candidates' intervals overlap.","Decide if a clear lead is supported and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.21-08","std": "MM.21","q": "A 10-year observational study finds that adults who buy organic groceries live 3 years longer on average than those who do not, and concludes organic food adds 3 years to your life. Critique this conclusion.","a": "Income, healthcare access, and overall lifestyle are confounding variables in an observational study, so causation cannot be established","lvl": 3,"choices": ["The study suffered from voluntary response bias only","Life expectancy is a categorical variable","10 years is too long for an observational study","Income, healthcare access, and overall lifestyle are confounding variables in an observational study, so causation cannot be established"],"steps": ["Check whether people were randomly assigned to buy organic food.","Think of lifestyle or income factors tied to both food and health.","Decide if causation is justified and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.21-09","std": "MM.21","q": "A phone maker tested 15 battery settings at α = 0.05 and advertised only the one with p = 0.03. Why is the claim weak?","a": "With 15 tests, a false positive is likely (about 54%); it needs replication","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Testing many settings always makes p-values more reliable","p = 0.03 is above 0.05, so it is not significant","15 tests is too few to find any effect","With 15 tests, a false positive is likely (about 54%); it needs replication"],"steps": ["Spot multiple testing (p-hacking) and hiding the 14 null results.","Find the chance of ≥ 1 false positive: 1 − (1 − 0.05)^{15}.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.22-01","std": "MM.22","q": "A student wants to randomly assign 24 volunteers equally into Treatment Group A and Control Group B. Which method is valid?","a": "Write all 24 names on identical slips, mix them in a hat, and draw 12 for Group A and the rest for Group B","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Put boys in Group A and girls in Group B","Write all 24 names on identical slips, mix them in a hat, and draw 12 for Group A and the rest for Group B","Put the first 12 volunteers who arrive into Group A","Let volunteers pick which group they want to join"],"steps": ["Valid random assignment gives every person an equal chance.","Eliminate methods using arrival order, choice, or gender.","Pick the true randomization method and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.22-02","std": "MM.22","q": "In a randomized experiment testing whether stretching for 5 minutes before a 100-meter sprint improves sprint time, what is the explanatory variable?","a": "Whether the runner stretches before sprinting","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Sprint time in seconds","Whether the runner stretches before sprinting","Number of runners","Length of the track"],"steps": ["The explanatory variable is the treatment you test or vary.","Ask which factor is changed to see its effect on sprint time.","Match the treatment variable to the choices and check."]},{"id": "MM.22-03","std": "MM.22","q": "When designing a student survey, why is it important to use neutral, non-leading question wording?","a": "To avoid wording bias that pushes respondents toward a specific answer","lvl": 1,"choices": ["To make the sample size larger","To eliminate all random sampling error","To avoid wording bias that pushes respondents toward a specific answer","To turn the survey into an experiment"],"steps": ["Think about how loaded words can nudge people's answers.","Neutral phrasing keeps responses honest rather than steered.","Match that reason to the choices and check your answer."]},{"id": "MM.22-04","std": "MM.22","q": "A student wants to sample students from a high school with 40 homerooms. She randomly selects 2 homerooms and surveys every student in those 2 rooms. What sampling method is this?","a": "Cluster sampling","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Simple random sampling","Cluster sampling","Voluntary response sampling","Stratified random sampling"],"steps": ["Notice that whole groups (homerooms) are picked at random.","Distinguish taking some from every group vs all from a few groups.","Match taking all students from 2 random rooms to the choices."]},{"id": "MM.22-05","std": "MM.22","q": "In a randomized experiment comparing two plant fertilizers on 30 identical tomato seedlings, why should all 30 seedlings receive the exact same amount of water and sunlight?","a": "To control extraneous variables so growth differences can be attributed to the fertilizer","lvl": 2,"choices": ["To control extraneous variables so growth differences can be attributed to the fertilizer","To double the sample size","To make the experiment double-blind","To reduce the number of treatments"],"steps": ["Ask why only the fertilizer should differ between groups.","Holding water and light steady prevents other growth causes.","Match that experimental control to the choices and check."]},{"id": "MM.22-06","std": "MM.22","q": "A student takes a simple random sample of 60 students from her school of 800 and finds 45% walk or bike to school (95% confidence interval: 32% to 58%). What is a valid conclusion?","a": "We are 95% confident that between 32% and 58% of all 800 students at the school walk or bike to school","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Exactly 45% of all 800 students walk or bike to school","Walking or biking causes better school attendance","Between 32% and 58% of all U.S. students walk or bike to school","We are 95% confident that between 32% and 58% of all 800 students at the school walk or bike to school"],"steps": ["Check what population the SRS came from and what a CI estimates.","Generalize only to the 800 students sampled, not the whole U.S.","Pick the valid confidence statement and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.22-07","std": "MM.22","q": "A student tests whether handwriting notes or typing notes leads to higher quiz scores by having an honors class handwrite and a standard class type. What is the design flaw and how should it be fixed?","a": "Class level is confounded with note-taking method; fix it by randomly assigning students within each class to handwrite or type","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Class level is confounded with note-taking method; fix it by randomly assigning students within each class to handwrite or type","Fix it by letting students choose whether to type or handwrite","Fix it by giving the honors class a harder quiz","There is no flaw because both classes take the same quiz"],"steps": ["Ask what differs between the two groups besides note-taking.","Think how to separate class level from the note-taking method.","Pick the critique and fix that match, then check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.22-08","std": "MM.22","q": "A student randomly assigns 30 volunteers from her PE class to do push-ups with upbeat music or in silence and finds the music group completes significantly more push-ups (p = 0.02). What is the proper scope of inference?","a": "Cause and effect can be concluded for these 30 volunteers, but results cannot be generalized to all teenagers because the subjects were not randomly sampled","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Results can be generalized to all teenagers, but causation cannot be concluded","Cause and effect can be concluded and generalized to all teenagers nationwide","Cause and effect can be concluded for these 30 volunteers, but results cannot be generalized to all teenagers because the subjects were not randomly sampled","Neither causation nor generalization is possible"],"steps": ["Separate random assignment (causation) from random sampling (scope).","Check which one this PE volunteer study used and which it lacked.","Pick the scope of inference matching both and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.22-09","std": "MM.22","q": "Which design tests whether cold water or room-temperature water affects typing speed?","a": "Randomly assign 40 students to each water, same test, compare means","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Randomly assign 40 students to each water, same test, compare means","Let 40 students pick their water, then compare means","Give all 40 students cold water and compare to last year","Test one student with each water and compare"],"steps": ["Plan subjects, random assignment to two water groups, and timing.","Keep keyboard, passage, and room identical to control confounding.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.1-10","std": "MM.1","q": "In the Mathematical Modeling Cycle, what is the primary purpose of the validation step after computing a solution from a mathematical model?","a": "To check whether the model's predictions make sense and align with real-world data","lvl": 1,"choices": ["To check whether the model's predictions make sense and align with real-world data","To eliminate all variables from the equation before graphing","To guarantee that no assumptions were made during the modeling process","To replace real-world data with theoretical estimates"]},{"id": "MM.1-11","std": "MM.1","q": "A student wants to determine whether high school athletes at her school sleep fewer hours per night than non-athletes by collecting a random survey and analyzing the results. Which problem-solving framework is most directly suited to this investigation?","a": "The Statistical Problem-Solving Cycle","lvl": 1,"choices": ["A deterministic three-dimensional scale model","The Statistical Problem-Solving Cycle","A straight-line depreciation schedule","A fixed-rate mortgage amortization table"]},{"id": "MM.1-12","std": "MM.1","q": "A student models the daily morning commute time of a school bus along a fixed route. Which factor is a variable in the model rather than a constant?","a": "Number of red traffic lights encountered on the trip","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Total road distance along the fixed bus route","Number of seconds in one minute","Number of red traffic lights encountered on the trip","Number of passenger seats installed on the bus"]},{"id": "MM.1-13","std": "MM.1","q": "A high school band hosts a pancake breakfast fundraiser. Renting the kitchen costs §140, ingredients cost §2.50 per meal, and tickets sell for §9.00 each. Using the profit model P = 9n − (140 + 2.50n), what is the minimum number of tickets they must sell to earn a profit of at least §250?","a": "60 tickets","lvl": 2,"choices": ["44 tickets","39 tickets","156 tickets","60 tickets"]},{"id": "MM.1-14","std": "MM.1","q": "A student models her smartphone battery percentage while streaming video as B(t) = 100 − 11.5t, where t is hours of streaming. After testing the model for 6 hours, her actual battery level is 25%. How does the model's prediction compare to the actual battery level?","a": "The model overestimates the actual battery level by 6 percentage points","lvl": 2,"choices": ["The model overestimates the actual battery level by 6 percentage points","The model underestimates the actual battery level by 6 percentage points","The model overestimates the actual battery level by 11.5 percentage points","The model matches the actual battery level exactly"]},{"id": "MM.1-15","std": "MM.1","q": "A food truck owner models daily taco demand as D = 180 − 20p, where p is the price per taco in dollars. At a price of §4 per taco, what daily revenue R = p × D does the model predict?","a": "§400","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§100","§400","§320","§520"]},{"id": "MM.1-16","std": "MM.1","q": "Two models predict the temperature (in °F) of a bowl of hot soup t minutes after being served in a 70°F room: Linear Model L(t) = 190 − 10t and Exponential Model E(t) = 70 + 120 × (0.5)^{t/5}. At t = 10 minutes, the measured soup temperature is 98°F. Which model is more accurate at t = 10 minutes and by how many degrees?","a": "Exponential Model E is more accurate by 6°F","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Linear Model L is more accurate by 6°F","Exponential Model E is more accurate by 10°F","Exponential Model E is more accurate by 6°F","Both models have the exact same error at t = 10 minutes"]},{"id": "MM.1-17","std": "MM.1","q": "A school robotics club builds a revenue model for selling 3D-printed keychains at a craft fair based on price and foot traffic. On the first day, they sell out of all 120 keychains in 2 hours while dozens of shoppers still want to buy one. What is the best revision in the next iteration of the Mathematical Modeling Cycle?","a": "Add a production/inventory capacity constraint to the model and test larger batch sizes or higher prices","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Discard the model because selling out means math cannot predict craft fair sales","Lower the price in the model so that demand decreases to match 120 keychains","Keep the model unchanged since 100% of inventory was sold","Add a production/inventory capacity constraint to the model and test larger batch sizes or higher prices"]},{"id": "MM.1-18","std": "MM.1","q": "A student wants to know whether a second car-rider pickup lane would meaningfully cut parent wait time. Which plan uses the Statistical Problem-Solving Cycle and the Mathematical Modeling Cycle correctly?","a": "Time a random sample of cars over several days; use variables such as arrival rate and loading time per lane; assume a constant arrival rate; check a 1-lane model against measured waits before predicting 2-lane savings","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Time a random sample of cars over several days; use variables such as arrival rate and loading time per lane; assume a constant arrival rate; check a 1-lane model against measured waits before predicting 2-lane savings","Ask parents whether they want a second lane and report the percentage who say yes","Assume two lanes cut every wait in half, so no data are needed","Time one car on one day and use that wait for every car"]},{"id": "MM.2-10","std": "MM.2","q": "A student uses the 50/30/20 budgeting rule (50% needs, 30% wants, 20% savings) on a monthly take-home income of §1,850. How much money is allocated to savings each month?","a": "§370","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§555","§370","§925","§185"]},{"id": "MM.2-11","std": "MM.2","q": "A cafe offers a monthly drink club for §12 per month plus §1.50 per iced coffee. Which linear model gives the total monthly cost C(n) for buying n iced coffees through the club?","a": "C(n) = 12 + 1.50n","lvl": 1,"choices": ["C(n) = 12n + 1.50","C(n) = 13.50n","C(n) = 12 + 1.50n","C(n) = 12 − 1.50n"]},{"id": "MM.2-12","std": "MM.2","q": "A sales associate earns a 6% commission on electronics sold. If she sells §3,500 worth of electronics in one week, how much commission does she earn?","a": "§210","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§21","§583.33","§3,710","§210"]},{"id": "MM.2-13","std": "MM.2","q": "A local movie theater offers a monthly pass for §24 per month plus §4 per ticket, while regular tickets cost §12 each without the pass. How many movie tickets in a month is the break-even point where both options cost the exact same amount?","a": "3 tickets","lvl": 2,"choices": ["3 tickets","2 tickets","4 tickets","6 tickets"]},{"id": "MM.2-14","std": "MM.2","q": "An online math tutor charges §35 per hour, pays a 10% platform service fee on gross earnings, and spends §28 per week on digital practice materials. If the tutor works 12 hours in a week, what is the tutor's net weekly profit?","a": "§350","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§378","§350","§352.80","§392"]},{"id": "MM.2-15","std": "MM.2","q": "Liam plans to graduate debt-free from a 24-month technical certificate program costing §11,400 in total. He wins a §3,000 scholarship and works part-time throughout all 24 months to pay the remaining balance in equal monthly amounts. How much must he pay each month?","a": "§350","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§475","§600","§350","§300"]},{"id": "MM.2-16","std": "MM.2","q": "A courier can choose Pay Plan A (§14 per hour plus §0.50 per delivery) or Pay Plan B (§10 per hour plus §1.50 per delivery). If the courier averages 5 deliveries per hour and works 20 hours in a week, which plan pays more for the week and by how much?","a": "Pay Plan B by §20","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Pay Plan A by §20","Pay Plan B by §80","Both plans pay the exact same amount","Pay Plan B by §20"]},{"id": "MM.2-17","std": "MM.2","q": "Priya's monthly net income is §2,600. Currently she spends §1,100 on fixed rent, §400 on groceries, and §180 on utilities, and saves §550 per month. Next year her rent and savings target stay unchanged, while both groceries and utilities are projected to increase by 5%. How much will she have left each month for all other discretionary spending next year?","a": "§341","lvl": 3,"choices": ["§341","§370","§286","§399"]},{"id": "MM.2-18","std": "MM.2","q": "A student buys a §3,600 mower, spends §15 per yard on operating costs, and charges §55 per yard. Which profit model and results are correct?","a": "P(x) = 40x − 3600; break-even at 90 yards; P(150) = §2,400; assumptions: costs stay §15 per yard and every yard pays §55","lvl": 3,"choices": ["P(x) = 55x − 3600; break-even at about 66 yards; P(150) = §4,650","P(x) = 40x − 3600; break-even at 90 yards; P(150) = §2,400; assumptions: costs stay §15 per yard and every yard pays §55","P(x) = 40x − 3600; break-even at 90 yards; P(150) = §6,000","P(x) = 40x + 3600; it never breaks even; P(150) = §9,600"]},{"id": "MM.3-10","std": "MM.3","q": "A financial table tracks the straight-line book value of a commercial refrigerator: Year 0: §4,200; Year 1: §3,650; Year 2: §3,100; Year 3: §2,550. What is the common difference of this arithmetic sequence?","a": "−§550 per year","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§550 per year","−§650 per year","−§550 per year","−§1,650 per year"]},{"id": "MM.3-11","std": "MM.3","q": "A certificate of deposit starts with §3,500 and earns 4% annual simple interest. How much interest is added to its value each year?","a": "§140","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§14","§1,400","§87.50","§140"]},{"id": "MM.3-12","std": "MM.3","q": "A studio buys a lighting kit for §1,500 and depreciates it by §250 each year using straight-line depreciation. Which explicit arithmetic sequence formula gives its value V(n) after n years (for 0 ≤ n ≤ 6)?","a": "V(n) = 1500 − 250n","lvl": 1,"choices": ["V(n) = 1500 − 250n","V(n) = 1500 + 250n","V(n) = 1500 × (0.75)^n","V(n) = 250 − 1500n"]},{"id": "MM.3-13","std": "MM.3","q": "A municipal bond earns annual simple interest. Its value is §5,000 at Year 0 and §6,350 at Year 6. What is the annual simple interest rate?","a": "4.5%","lvl": 2,"choices": ["2.7%","4.5%","27.0%","5.4%"]},{"id": "MM.3-14","std": "MM.3","q": "A dental clinic purchases an X-ray machine for §48,000 and depreciates it using straight-line depreciation over 10 years to a salvage value of §6,000. What is its book value at the end of Year 7?","a": "§18,600","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§14,400","§29,400","§18,600","§22,800"]},{"id": "MM.3-15","std": "MM.3","q": "A savings note begins with §2,400 and earns 7.5% annual simple interest. After how many years will its total value reach §3,480?","a": "6 years","lvl": 2,"choices": ["5 years","8 years","19 years","6 years"]},{"id": "MM.3-16","std": "MM.3","q": "A spreadsheet models the straight-line depreciation of a CNC router. Its book value is §21,500 at Year 3 and §14,000 at Year 8. What was the original purchase price of the router at Year 0?","a": "§26,000","lvl": 3,"choices": ["§26,000","§29,000","§24,500","§23,000"]},{"id": "MM.3-17","std": "MM.3","q": "Company A buys a forklift for §32,000 and depreciates it straight-line to §4,000 over 7 years. Company B buys a forklift for §28,000 and depreciates it straight-line to §4,000 over 8 years. After how many years will both forklifts have the exact same book value?","a": "4 years","lvl": 3,"choices": ["2 years","4 years","3 years","6 years"]},{"id": "MM.3-18","std": "MM.3","q": "A theater invests §15,000 at 4.8% simple interest for 5 years and buys a §9,200 sound board that depreciates straight-line to §1,200 over 5 years. Which formulas and combined Year 5 value are correct?","a": "A(n) = 15000 + 720n and V(n) = 9200 − 1600n; combined value §19,800","lvl": 3,"choices": ["A(n) = 15000 + 720n and V(n) = 9200 − 1840n; combined value §18,600","A(n) = 15000 + 72n and V(n) = 9200 − 1600n; combined value §16,560","A(n) = 15000 + 720n and V(n) = 9200 − 1600n; combined value §19,800","A(n) = 15000 + 720n and V(n) = 9200 − 1600n; combined value §27,800"]},{"id": "MM.4-10","std": "MM.4","q": "A geometric sequence models the yearly value of a collectible comic book appreciating at 8% per year: §500.00, §540.00, §583.20, ... What is the common ratio of this geometric sequence?","a": "1.08","lvl": 1,"choices": ["0.08","0.92","40","1.08"]},{"id": "MM.4-11","std": "MM.4","q": "A tablet purchased for §800 loses 25% of its value each year under proportional depreciation. What is the common ratio of the geometric sequence representing its yearly value?","a": "0.75","lvl": 1,"choices": ["0.75","0.25","1.25","−0.25"]},{"id": "MM.4-12","std": "MM.4","part": "a","q": "When comparing compound interest accounts by writing each growth model in the annual form A = P(1 + r)^t (with t in years), what financial rate does r represent?","a": "The annual percentage yield (APY)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["The periodic monthly interest rate","The annual percentage yield (APY)","The simple interest dollar amount","The total interest earned after t years"]},{"id": "MM.4-13","std": "MM.4","q": "An investor deposits §3,000 into an account earning 5.2% APR compounded weekly (52 times per year). What is the balance after 2 years, rounded to the nearest cent?","a": "§3,328.63","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§3,312.00","§3,320.11","§3,328.63","§3,160.05"]},{"id": "MM.4-14","std": "MM.4","part": "a","q": "An investment pays 8% APR compounded continuously, modeled by A = P × e^{0.08t}. What is the equivalent APY (value of r in A = P(1 + r)^t), rounded to the nearest hundredth of a percent?","a": "8.33%","lvl": 2,"choices": ["8.00%","8.30%","8.64%","8.33%"]},{"id": "MM.4-15","std": "MM.4","q": "A commercial mapping drone costs §4,500 and depreciates proportionally by 18% each year. What is its value after 3 years, rounded to the nearest cent?","a": "§2,481.16","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§2,481.16","§2,070.00","§3,025.80","§2,624.40"]},{"id": "MM.4-16","std": "MM.4","q": "Maya invests §6,000 for 5 years. Account X pays 6% APR compounded monthly, while Account Y pays 6% APR compounded weekly (52 times per year). How much more does Account Y have than Account X after 5 years, rounded to the nearest cent?","a": "§4.65","lvl": 3,"choices": ["§0.00","§4.65","§19.86","§293.10"]},{"id": "MM.4-17","std": "MM.4","part": "a","q": "A high-yield savings account compounds monthly and advertises an APY of 5.12%. What nominal APR (rounded to the nearest hundredth of a percent) corresponds to this APY?","a": "5.00%","lvl": 3,"choices": ["5.12%","5.24%","5.00%","4.88%"]},{"id": "MM.4-18","std": "MM.4","part": "a","q": "An online bank offers 4.8% APR compounded weekly; a local bank offers 4.75% APR compounded continuously. Which comparison is correct?","a": "Online APY ≈ 4.91% and local APY ≈ 4.86%; APY puts both in the form A = P(1 + r)^t, so the online bank yields more","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Online APY = 4.80% and local APY = 4.75%; the local bank yields more because it compounds continuously","Online APY ≈ 4.91% and local APY ≈ 4.86%; the local bank yields more because continuous compounding always wins","Online APY ≈ 5.20% and local APY ≈ 4.86%; APR and APY are the same thing","Online APY ≈ 4.91% and local APY ≈ 4.86%; APY puts both in the form A = P(1 + r)^t, so the online bank yields more"]},{"id": "MM.5-10","std": "MM.5","q": "Two accounts each start with §2,500. Account A earns 6% simple interest per year, and Account B earns 6% interest compounded annually. How do their balances compare at the end of Year 1?","a": "They are equal (§2,650 in both accounts)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["They are equal (§2,650 in both accounts)","Account B has §9 more than Account A","Account A has §150 more than Account B","Account B has §150 more than Account A"]},{"id": "MM.5-11","std": "MM.5","q": "When graphed against time in years, how does the graph of straight-line depreciation compare to the graph of proportional depreciation?","a": "Straight-line depreciation is a downward-sloping straight line, while proportional depreciation is a decreasing curve that flattens over time","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Both graphs are straight lines with different negative slopes","Straight-line depreciation is a downward-sloping straight line, while proportional depreciation is a decreasing curve that flattens over time","Straight-line depreciation curves downward, while proportional depreciation is a straight line","Proportional depreciation drops below §0 faster than straight-line depreciation"]},{"id": "MM.5-12","std": "MM.5","q": "A §6,000 boat motor loses §1,200 in Year 1 under both straight-line depreciation (§1,200/year) and proportional depreciation (20%/year). How do the dollar amounts lost during Year 2 compare?","a": "Straight-line loses §1,200, while proportional loses §960","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Both models lose §1,200 during Year 2","Straight-line loses §960, while proportional loses §1,200","Straight-line loses §1,200, while proportional loses §960","Straight-line loses §1,200, while proportional loses §240"]},{"id": "MM.5-13","std": "MM.5","q": "Two savings plans each start with §8,000. Plan A pays 4% simple interest per year, and Plan B pays 4% interest compounded semiannually. How much more is in Plan B than in Plan A after 2 years, rounded to the nearest cent?","a": "§19.46","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§12.80","§32.00","§659.46","§19.46"]},{"id": "MM.5-14","std": "MM.5","q": "A commercial espresso machine costs §9,000. Model 1 uses straight-line depreciation of §1,500 per year, while Model 2 uses proportional depreciation of 25% per year. After 2 years, which model gives the higher book value and by how much?","a": "Model 1 is higher by §937.50","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Model 1 is higher by §937.50","Model 2 is higher by §937.50","Model 1 is higher by §1,500.00","Model 2 is higher by §562.50"]},{"id": "MM.5-15","std": "MM.5","q": "Accounts X and Y start with the same principal and the same positive annual interest rate. Account X earns simple interest and earns §300 during Year 4. Account Y earns interest compounded annually. What must be true about the interest Account Y earns during Year 4?","a": "Account Y earns more than §300 during Year 4 because interest is calculated on the principal plus all prior interest","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Account Y also earns §300 during Year 4 because the annual interest rate is the same","Account Y earns more than §300 during Year 4 because interest is calculated on the principal plus all prior interest","Account Y earns less than §300 during Year 4 because compounding slows down over time","Account Y earns exactly 4 times §300 during Year 4"]},{"id": "MM.5-16","std": "MM.5","q": "Fund A invests §10,000 at 8% simple interest per year. Fund B invests §10,000 at 6% interest compounded annually. At the end of which full year does Fund B's balance first exceed Fund A's balance?","a": "Year 11","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Year 9","Year 10","Year 11","Year 12"]},{"id": "MM.5-17","std": "MM.5","q": "A landscaper buys a §16,000 woodchipper. Straight-line depreciation reduces its value by §2,000 per year, while proportional depreciation reduces its value by 20% per year. During Years 1 through 5, in which year is the gap (straight-line value minus proportional value) largest, and what is that difference?","a": "Year 3, with a difference of §1,808","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Year 1, with a difference of §1,200","Year 2, with a difference of §1,760","Year 4, with a difference of §1,446.40","Year 3, with a difference of §1,808"]},{"id": "MM.5-18","std": "MM.5","q": "Compare §1,000 invested for 20 years at 7% simple interest and at 7% compounded annually. Which statement is correct?","a": "Simple: §2,400, earning §70 in both Year 1 and Year 20; compound: §3,869.68, earning §70 in Year 1 and §253.16 in Year 20","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Simple: §2,400, earning §70 in both Year 1 and Year 20; compound: §3,869.68, earning §70 in Year 1 and §253.16 in Year 20","Simple: §2,400; compound: §3,869.68, earning §70 in every year","Simple: §1,400; compound: §3,869.68, earning §253.16 in Year 20","Simple: §2,400, earning §1,400 in Year 20; compound: §3,869.68"]},{"id": "MM.6-10","std": "MM.6","q": "A credit card has a beginning balance of §940. During the billing cycle, the cardholder returns a §110 jacket (receiving a merchant credit) and makes a §230 payment, with no new purchases or fees. Before any interest is added, what is the adjusted balance?","a": "§600","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§820","§600","§1,060","§710"]},{"id": "MM.6-11","std": "MM.6","q": "In a monthly credit card payoff spreadsheet, Month 1's ending balance is calculated in cell G2. What formula should be entered in cell B3 to automatically pull Month 2's beginning balance?","a": "=G2","lvl": 1,"choices": ["=B2","=SUM(B2:G2)","=G2","=G2-B2"]},{"id": "MM.6-12","std": "MM.6","q": "A credit card sets its minimum monthly payment to 3% of the statement balance or §25, whichever is greater. If the statement balance is §1,400, what is the minimum payment?","a": "§42","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§25","§67","§420","§42"]},{"id": "MM.6-13","std": "MM.6","q": "A credit card has a 21.9% APR and uses a 365-day year to calculate its daily periodic rate. If a cardholder's average daily balance during a 30-day billing cycle is §1,500, what is the finance charge for that cycle?","a": "§27.00","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§27.00","§0.90","§27.38","§328.50"]},{"id": "MM.6-14","std": "MM.6","q": "A cardholder transfers a §3,000 balance to a card offering 0% promotional APR for 12 months with a one-time 4% balance transfer fee added to the balance. What equal monthly payment pays off the entire debt in 12 months?","a": "§260","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§250","§260","§240","§370"]},{"id": "MM.6-15","std": "MM.6","q": "Row 2 of a credit card spreadsheet shows: Beginning Balance = §750, Merchant Credits = §50, Payment = §150, New Purchases = §220, Annual Fee = §45. A 1.8% monthly finance charge is calculated on (Beginning Balance − Credits − Payment). What is the Ending Balance for Row 2?","a": "§824.90","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§815.00","§828.50","§824.90","§829.67"]},{"id": "MM.6-16","std": "MM.6","q": "In a credit card spreadsheet, column B is Beginning Balance, column C is Merchant Credits, and column D is Payment. If the card's minimum payment rule is the greater of §30 or 4% of (Beginning Balance − Credits), which formula correctly belongs in cell D2?","a": "=MAX(30, 0.04(B2-C2))","lvl": 3,"choices": ["=MIN(30, 0.04(B2-C2))","=30+0.04(B2-C2)","=MAX(30, 0.04(B2+C2))","=MAX(30, 0.04(B2-C2))"]},{"id": "MM.6-17","std": "MM.6","q": "A cardholder carries a §1,600 balance at 18% APR (1.5% monthly interest added to the beginning balance before each payment) with no new purchases. Compare paying §100 per month versus §400 per month over the first 2 months. How much less total interest is charged over those 2 months under the §400/month plan?","a": "§4.50","lvl": 3,"choices": ["§4.50","§0.00","§9.00","§42.36"]},{"id": "MM.6-18","std": "MM.6","q": "A §1,000 card balance at 2% monthly interest gets interest added first, then the minimum payment of 3% of the balance after interest (or §25, whichever is greater). What are the results for Months 1 and 2?","a": "Month 1: §20.00 interest, §30.60 payment, §989.40 ending; Month 2: §19.79 interest, §30.28 payment, §978.91 ending","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Month 1: §20.00 interest, §30.00 payment, §990.00 ending; Month 2: §19.80 interest, §29.70 payment, §980.10 ending","Month 1: §20.00 interest, §30.60 payment, §989.40 ending; Month 2: §19.79 interest, §30.28 payment, §978.91 ending","Month 1: §20.00 interest, §25.00 payment, §995.00 ending; Month 2: §19.90 interest, §25.00 payment, §989.90 ending","Month 1: §240.00 interest, §37.20 payment, §1,202.80 ending; Month 2: §288.67 interest, §44.75 payment, §1,446.72 ending"]},{"id": "MM.7-10","std": "MM.7","q": "What is a major financial advantage of purchasing a house with cash rather than taking out a 30-year mortgage?","a": "The buyer avoids paying mortgage interest and loan origination fees over the life of the loan","lvl": 1,"choices": ["The buyer no longer has to pay property taxes or homeowner's insurance","The home is guaranteed to double in resale value in 5 years","The buyer avoids paying mortgage interest and loan origination fees over the life of the loan","The buyer keeps all cash liquid in a savings account"]},{"id": "MM.7-11","std": "MM.7","part": "a","q": "Which mortgage product starts with a lower fixed introductory interest rate for a set period (such as 5 years) before the interest rate can adjust periodically based on market rates?","a": "Adjustable-rate mortgage (ARM)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["15-year fixed-rate mortgage","Reverse mortgage","Interest-free cash purchase","Adjustable-rate mortgage (ARM)"]},{"id": "MM.7-12","std": "MM.7","part": "b","q": "Holding the home purchase price, interest rate, and loan term constant, how does making a larger down payment affect the monthly mortgage payment?","a": "It decreases the monthly payment because a smaller principal amount is borrowed","lvl": 1,"choices": ["It decreases the monthly payment because a smaller principal amount is borrowed","It increases the monthly payment because more cash is used upfront","It has no effect on the monthly payment","It shortens the loan term automatically to 15 years"]},{"id": "MM.7-13","std": "MM.7","part": "b","q": "On a §240,000 30-year fixed mortgage, a 6.0% APR gives a monthly payment of §1,438.92, while a 7.0% APR gives a monthly payment of §1,596.73. How much more total interest does the 7.0% loan cost over 30 years?","a": "§56,811.60","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§4,734.30","§56,811.60","§157.81","§28,405.80"]},{"id": "MM.7-14","std": "MM.7","q": "A family signs a 3-year lease-to-purchase contract paying §2,100 per month (whereas comparable rentals cost §1,750 per month), with the extra §350 per month credited toward buying the home. If they decide NOT to buy at the end of 3 years and forfeit the credits, how much extra did they pay above market rent?","a": "§12,600","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§4,200","§75,600","§12,600","§63,000"]},{"id": "MM.7-15","std": "MM.7","part": "a","q": "An eligible veteran compares a VA mortgage (0% down, no monthly PMI, 2.15% one-time funding fee) against a conventional loan on a §300,000 home. What is the dollar amount of the VA funding fee on a §300,000 loan?","a": "§6,450","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§645","§64,500","§2,150","§6,450"]},{"id": "MM.7-16","std": "MM.7","part": "b","q": "A buyer borrows §250,000 at 6.0% APR. A 30-year mortgage has a monthly payment of §1,498.88, while a 20-year mortgage has a monthly payment of §1,791.08. How much total interest does the 20-year mortgage save over the life of the loan compared to the 30-year mortgage?","a": "§109,737.60","lvl": 3,"choices": ["§109,737.60","§70,128.00","§179,859.20","§289,596.80"]},{"id": "MM.7-17","std": "MM.7","part": "c","q": "Jordan can rent for §1,500/month or buy a home requiring §40,000 upfront and §2,150/month in total ownership costs. Suppose Jordan rents instead, invests the §40,000 upfront at 6% compounded annually for 4 years, and sets aside the §650/month cost difference in cash (ignoring interest on the monthly deposits) for 4 years. What is Jordan's combined investment and cash total after 4 years, rounded to the nearest dollar?","a": "§81,699","lvl": 3,"choices": ["§50,499","§81,699","§71,200","§80,800"]},{"id": "MM.7-18","std": "MM.7","part": "c","q": "Renting costs §1,600/month; buying costs §2,200/month plus §30,000 upfront, and the home rises from §260,000 to §300,000 in 5 years. Which analysis is correct?","a": "Buying builds equity through appreciation and principal paydown; renting frees §600/month and §30,000 to invest with no upkeep or selling costs; moving after 2 years favors renting because closing and selling costs outweigh 2 years of gains","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Moving after 2 years favors buying because the home always gains §40,000","Buying is always better because homes appreciate, so renting has no advantages","Buying builds equity through appreciation and principal paydown; renting frees §600/month and §30,000 to invest with no upkeep or selling costs; moving after 2 years favors renting because closing and selling costs outweigh 2 years of gains","Renting is always better because rent never increases"]},{"id": "MM.8-10","std": "MM.8","q": "When a 36-month automobile lease ends and the driver returns the car to the dealership, how much ownership equity does the driver have in the vehicle?","a": "§0","lvl": 1,"choices": ["The total of all 36 monthly lease payments","Half of the car's original sticker price","The amount paid at signing","§0"]},{"id": "MM.8-11","std": "MM.8","q": "A buyer purchases a §19,500 sedan using a §3,500 trade-in and §2,000 in cash down payment. How much remains to be financed with an auto loan?","a": "§14,000","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§14,000","§16,000","§17,500","§25,000"]},{"id": "MM.8-12","std": "MM.8","q": "What is a key disadvantage of paying §30,000 cash for a new car instead of taking a low-interest auto loan?","a": "It ties up a large amount of cash that could otherwise earn investment returns or serve as an emergency fund","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Cash buyers must pay higher monthly interest charges","It ties up a large amount of cash that could otherwise earn investment returns or serve as an emergency fund","Cash buyers cannot sell the vehicle for 5 years","Cash buyers must pay excess mileage penalties"]},{"id": "MM.8-13","std": "MM.8","q": "A dealership offers two choices on a §26,000 car (with §0 down): Deal 1 is 0% APR for 60 months with no rebate; Deal 2 is a §2,000 cash rebate and financing the remaining §24,000 at §442 per month for 60 months. Which deal costs less in total and by how much?","a": "Deal 1 costs §520 less","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Deal 2 costs §520 less","Deal 2 costs §2,000 less","Deal 1 costs §520 less","Deal 1 costs §1,480 less"]},{"id": "MM.8-14","std": "MM.8","q": "A 36-month car lease requires §285 per month, §1,800 due at signing, a §395 lease-end disposition fee, and §450 in excess wear-and-tear fees. What is the total out-of-pocket cost of the lease?","a": "§12,905","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§12,060","§10,260","§12,510","§12,905"]},{"id": "MM.8-15","std": "MM.8","q": "Marcus pays §24,000 cash for a car instead of leaving that §24,000 in a certificate of deposit earning 5% compounded annually for 4 years. What is the investment interest he gives up (opportunity cost), rounded to the nearest cent?","a": "§5,172.15","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§5,172.15","§4,800.00","§29,172.15","§1,200.00"]},{"id": "MM.8-16","std": "MM.8","q": "Compare two 6-year transportation plans. Plan A: Lease two consecutive 36-month cars, each costing §340/month plus §2,200 due at signing. Plan B: Buy one car with §5,000 down and §460/month for 60 months, drive it payment-free in Year 6, and sell it at the end of Year 6 for §11,500. Which plan has the lower net 6-year cost and by how much?","a": "Plan B by §7,780","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Plan A by §3,720","Plan B by §7,780","Plan B by §3,720","Plan A by §7,780"]},{"id": "MM.8-17","std": "MM.8","q": "A buyer finances §30,000 for 60 months at §580/month with §0 down. After 24 months, the remaining loan payoff balance is §19,100, while the car's market resale value has depreciated to §16,800. What is the buyer's equity position if they sell the car at Month 24?","a": "−§2,300 (negative equity of §2,300)","lvl": 3,"choices": ["§2,300 in positive equity","−§13,200 (negative equity of §13,200)","−§2,300 (negative equity of §2,300)","§10,900 in positive equity"]},{"id": "MM.8-18","std": "MM.8","q": "A graduate drives 9,000 miles a year, wants low predictable payments under warranty, and likes a new car every 3 years. Which analysis is correct?","a": "Leasing gives lower payments and warranty coverage within her mileage, but no equity and extra fees; if she keeps a car 10 years, buying is better because of payment-free years and resale value","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Leasing is better even for 10 years because the payments stay low forever","Leasing builds equity, while buying has lower monthly payments","Leasing will not work because 9,000 miles a year always exceeds lease limits","Leasing gives lower payments and warranty coverage within her mileage, but no equity and extra fees; if she keeps a car 10 years, buying is better because of payment-free years and resale value"]},{"id": "MM.9-10","std": "MM.9","q": "A conical paper water cup has a radius of 4 cm and a height of 9 cm. What is its volume rounded to the nearest hundredth?","a": "150.80 cm³","lvl": 1,"choices": ["150.80 cm³","452.39 cm³","113.10 cm³","37.70 cm³"]},{"id": "MM.9-11","std": "MM.9","q": "A spherical decorative garden globe has a radius of 6 inches. What is its surface area rounded to the nearest hundredth?","a": "452.39 in²","lvl": 1,"choices": ["113.10 in²","452.39 in²","904.78 in²","226.19 in²"]},{"id": "MM.9-12","std": "MM.9","q": "A camping tent is shaped like a triangular prism. Its triangular front face has a base of 6 ft and a height of 4 ft, and the tent is 8 ft long. What is the interior volume of the tent?","a": "96 ft³","lvl": 1,"choices": ["192 ft³","48 ft³","96 ft³","18 ft³"]},{"id": "MM.9-13","std": "MM.9","q": "A cylindrical grain silo has a radius of 10 ft and a vertical wall height of 30 ft, topped by a hemispherical dome of radius 10 ft. What is the total interior volume of the silo, rounded to the nearest cubic foot?","a": "11,519 ft³","lvl": 2,"choices": ["9,425 ft³","13,614 ft³","10,472 ft³","11,519 ft³"]},{"id": "MM.9-14","std": "MM.9","q": "A shipping company designs a closed cylindrical mailing tube with radius 2 inches and length 18 inches. If cardboard costs §0.005 per square inch, what is the material cost for one tube, rounded to the nearest cent?","a": "§1.26","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§1.26","§1.13","§0.63","§1.38"]},{"id": "MM.9-15","std": "MM.9","q": "A rectangular glass aquarium has interior dimensions of 30 inches long, 12 inches wide, and 18 inches tall. If it is filled with water to a depth of 16 inches and 1 gallon equals 231 cubic inches, how many gallons of water does the aquarium hold, rounded to the nearest tenth?","a": "24.9 gallons","lvl": 2,"choices": ["28.1 gallons","24.9 gallons","3.1 gallons","16.6 gallons"]},{"id": "MM.9-16","std": "MM.9","q": "Three tennis balls, each with a diameter of 2.6 inches, fit snugly stacked end-to-end inside a closed cylindrical canister of diameter 2.6 inches and height 7.8 inches. What percentage of the canister's interior volume is empty air space, rounded to the nearest tenth of a percent?","a": "33.3%","lvl": 3,"choices": ["66.7%","25.0%","33.3%","50.0%"]},{"id": "MM.9-17","std": "MM.9","q": "A toy designer compares two solid wooden blocks that each have a 6 cm by 6 cm square base and a height of 10 cm: Design A is a square prism and Design B is a square pyramid. If wood costs §0.02 per cubic centimeter, how much less does the wood cost to manufacture 50 pyramid blocks compared to 50 prism blocks?","a": "§240","lvl": 3,"choices": ["§120","§360","§180","§240"]},{"id": "MM.9-18","std": "MM.9","q": "A raised garden bed uses exactly 24 feet of 1.5-foot-tall border planks. Which model and result are correct?","a": "V(w) = 1.5w(12 − w); a 6 ft by 6 ft bed gives the maximum of 54 ft³; a narrower bed may be easier to reach","lvl": 3,"choices": ["V(w) = 1.5w(12 − w); a 6 ft by 6 ft bed gives the maximum of 54 ft³; a narrower bed may be easier to reach","V(w) = 1.5w(24 − w); a 12 ft by 12 ft bed gives 216 ft³","V(w) = w(12 − w); a 6 ft by 6 ft bed gives 36 ft³","V(w) = 1.5w(12 − w); a 4 ft by 8 ft bed gives the maximum of 48 ft³"]},{"id": "MM.10-10","std": "MM.10","part": "a","q": "A machinist needs to measure the thread diameter of a small metal bolt to draft a 2D technical drawing for a replacement nut. Which measuring tool and level of precision is most appropriate?","a": "A digital caliper to the nearest 0.01 mm","lvl": 1,"choices": ["A surveyor's wheel to the nearest foot","A digital caliper to the nearest 0.01 mm","A yardstick to the nearest 1/2 inch","A laser rangefinder to the nearest 0.1 meter"]},{"id": "MM.10-11","std": "MM.10","part": "b","q": "A stepped wooden podium has a rectangular base 8 ft wide and 2 ft tall with a centered upper block 4 ft wide and 3 ft tall on top of it. What are the base width and maximum height shown in a front elevation drawing of the podium?","a": "Base width 8 ft and maximum height 5 ft","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Base width 12 ft and maximum height 5 ft","Base width 8 ft and maximum height 3 ft","Base width 8 ft and maximum height 5 ft","Base width 4 ft and maximum height 5 ft"]},{"id": "MM.10-12","std": "MM.10","part": "c","q": "When preparing a 2D side elevation drawing of a tall cylindrical water tower standing on a fenced lot, which measurement CANNOT be taken directly with a tape measure from the ground and must be calculated indirectly?","a": "The vertical height from the ground to the top of the tower","lvl": 1,"choices": ["The circumference of the concrete base at ground level","The width of the chain-link entrance gate","The spacing between fence posts along the lot perimeter","The vertical height from the ground to the top of the tower"]},{"id": "MM.10-13","std": "MM.10","part": "d","q": "A packaging engineer wants to show a factory exactly where to cut, score, and fold a single flat sheet of cardboard to assemble a hexagonal gift box. Which 2D visual representation is most appropriate?","a": "A dimensioned 2D net (flat pattern) showing cut lines and fold lines","lvl": 2,"choices": ["A dimensioned 2D net (flat pattern) showing cut lines and fold lines","A shaded 3D perspective rendering of the closed box","A top-view floor plan only","A histogram of box dimensions"]},{"id": "MM.10-14","std": "MM.10","part": "b","q": "A square gazebo has a 14 ft by 14 ft floor, vertical corner posts 9 ft tall, and a square-pyramid roof rising 5 ft above the tops of the posts. What 2D shapes and dimensions appear in a front elevation drawing?","a": "A 14 ft by 9 ft rectangle topped by an isosceles triangle with base 14 ft and height 5 ft","lvl": 2,"choices": ["A 14 ft by 14 ft square with diagonal lines meeting at the center","A 14 ft by 9 ft rectangle topped by an isosceles triangle with base 14 ft and height 5 ft","A 14 ft by 9 ft rectangle topped by a 14 ft by 5 ft rectangle","A 14 ft by 14 ft square topped by a triangle of height 9 ft"]},{"id": "MM.10-15","std": "MM.10","part": "c","q": "A carpenter draws a side elevation of a straight staircase that rises 9 feet vertically across a horizontal run of 12 feet. Before cutting the diagonal wooden stringer, what is its calculated length?","a": "15 ft","lvl": 2,"choices": ["21 ft","10.5 ft","15 ft","18 ft"]},{"id": "MM.10-16","std": "MM.10","part": "a","q": "A student draws a 2D front elevation of a gymnasium wall that is 96 ft wide and 28 ft tall on an 8.5 in by 11 in sheet of paper (landscape orientation), leaving at least a 0.5-inch margin on all four sides (usable area: 10 in wide by 7.5 in tall). Which scale produces the largest drawing that still fits within the margins?","a": "1 inch = 10 feet","lvl": 3,"choices": ["1 inch = 8 feet","1 inch = 12 feet","1 inch = 20 feet","1 inch = 10 feet"]},{"id": "MM.10-17","std": "MM.10","part": "c","q": "To draft an elevation drawing of a radio antenna mounted on a flat roof, an observer stands 60 ft horizontally from the building wall. From an eye height of 5.5 ft, the angle of elevation to the roofline is 25° and to the top of the antenna is 42°. What is the calculated height of the antenna alone (above the roof), rounded to the nearest tenth of a foot?","a": "26.0 ft","lvl": 3,"choices": ["26.0 ft","31.5 ft","54.0 ft","18.3 ft"]},{"id": "MM.10-18","std": "MM.10","part": "d","q": "A city council presents a proposed footbridge to engineers and to residents. Which plan is best?","a": "Engineers get exact dimensioned plan and elevation views; residents get 3D renderings; a diagonal brace or the span over the river must be calculated indirectly","lvl": 3,"choices": ["One elevation drawing serves both groups; the deck width must be calculated indirectly","Engineers get exact dimensioned plan and elevation views; residents get 3D renderings; a diagonal brace or the span over the river must be calculated indirectly","Engineers get 3D renderings; residents get dimensioned elevation views","Both groups get the same 3D rendering; the handrail height must be calculated indirectly"]},{"id": "MM.11-10","std": "MM.11","part": "a","q": "Into how many regions (called octants) do the three coordinate planes (xy-, xz-, and yz-planes) divide three-dimensional space?","a": "8 octants","lvl": 1,"choices": ["4 octants","6 octants","8 octants","12 octants"]},{"id": "MM.11-11","std": "MM.11","part": "a","q": "Which of the following points lies directly on the yz-plane in a three-dimensional Cartesian coordinate system?","a": "(0, −7, 4)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["(−7, 0, 4)","(4, −7, 0)","(1, 0, 0)","(0, −7, 4)"]},{"id": "MM.11-12","std": "MM.11","part": "b","q": "A point P(5, −2, −6) is graphed as the vertex opposite the origin of a right rectangular prism with edges along the x-, y-, and z-axes. What are the coordinates of the prism's vertex located on the y-axis?","a": "(0, −2, 0)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["(0, −2, 0)","(5, 0, 0)","(0, 0, −6)","(5, −2, 0)"]},{"id": "MM.11-13","std": "MM.11","part": "c","q": "In a theater lighting grid (coordinates in feet), a spotlight is mounted at (1, 3, 12) and a stage prop sits at (3, 9, 9). What is the straight-line distance from the spotlight to the prop?","a": "7 feet","lvl": 2,"choices": ["11 feet","7 feet","49 feet","5 feet"]},{"id": "MM.11-14","std": "MM.11","part": "d","q": "Two underwater temperature sensors are moored at coordinates (−5, 12, −18) and (9, −4, −6) (in meters). What are the coordinates of the midpoint where a relay buoy should be placed halfway between them?","a": "(2, 4, −12)","lvl": 2,"choices": ["(4, 8, −24)","(7, −8, 6)","(2, 4, −12)","(−7, 8, −6)"]},{"id": "MM.11-15","std": "MM.11","part": "b","q": "A right rectangular prism has one vertex at the origin (0, 0, 0), edges along the coordinate axes, and its opposite vertex at P(−6, −3, 2). What is the length of the space diagonal from the origin to P?","a": "7 units","lvl": 2,"choices": ["11 units","49 units","5 units","7 units"]},{"id": "MM.11-16","std": "MM.11","part": "c","q": "In a robotics lab (coordinates in meters), a charging dock sits at D(2, 1, 0). Robot A is positioned at (8, 5, 12) and Robot B is positioned at (−4, 9, 0). Which robot is closer to the charging dock and by how many meters?","a": "Robot B is closer by 4 meters","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Robot B is closer by 4 meters","Robot A is closer by 4 meters","Robot B is closer by 2 meters","Both robots are the same distance from the dock"]},{"id": "MM.11-17","std": "MM.11","part": "d","q": "A delivery drone flies in a straight line from launch pad A(−4, 2, 1) to rooftop B(8, 10, 13). It hovers at midpoint M of AB for a sensor scan, then flies to checkpoint N located at the midpoint between M and B. What are the coordinates of checkpoint N?","a": "(5, 8, 10)","lvl": 3,"choices": ["(2, 6, 7)","(5, 8, 10)","(4, 6, 9)","(6, 9, 11)"]},{"id": "MM.11-18","std": "MM.11","part": "c","q": "Access points sit at A(0, 0, 14), B(12, 0, 14), and C(6, 13, 14), and a server is at S(6, 4, 2) (meters). Which results are correct?","a": "SA = 14 m, SB = 14 m, SC = 15 m; total 43 m; midpoint of AB is (6, 0, 14)","lvl": 3,"choices": ["SA = 14 m, SB = 14 m, SC = 13 m; total 41 m; midpoint of AB is (6, 0, 14)","SA = 196 m, SB = 196 m, SC = 225 m; total 617 m; midpoint of AB is (6, 0, 14)","SA = 14 m, SB = 14 m, SC = 15 m; total 43 m; midpoint of AB is (6, 0, 14)","SA = 14 m, SB = 14 m, SC = 15 m; total 43 m; midpoint of AB is (12, 0, 28)"]},{"id": "MM.12-10","std": "MM.12","q": "A delivery drone positioned at (3, −5, 8) in a 3D coordinate system translates 4 units in the positive x-direction, 7 units in the positive y-direction, and 3 units in the negative z-direction. What are the drone's new coordinates?","a": "(7, 2, 5)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["(7, −12, 11)","(−1, 2, 11)","(7, 2, 11)","(7, 2, 5)"]},{"id": "MM.12-11","std": "MM.12","q": "In a 3D CAD model, a vertex located at (−4, 6, −9) is reflected across the xz-plane. What are the coordinates of its image?","a": "(−4, −6, −9)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["(−4, −6, −9)","(4, 6, 9)","(4, −6, 9)","(−4, 6, 9)"]},{"id": "MM.12-12","std": "MM.12","q": "A corner of a 3D sculpture at (−8, 4, 10) is dilated from the origin by a scale factor of 3/2. What are the new coordinates of the corner?","a": "(−12, 6, 15)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["(12, −6, −15)","(−12, 6, 15)","(−6.5, 5.5, 11.5)","(−16/3, 8/3, 20/3)"]},{"id": "MM.12-13","std": "MM.12","q": "In a right-handed 3D coordinate system, the tip of a robotic arm at (4, −7, 5) is rotated 90° counterclockwise about the z-axis (when viewed from the positive z-axis toward the origin). What are the new coordinates of the tip?","a": "(7, 4, 5)","lvl": 2,"choices": ["(−7, −4, 5)","(−4, 7, 5)","(7, 4, 5)","(4, −7, −5)"]},{"id": "MM.12-14","std": "MM.12","q": "A steel bracket corner at (2, −3, 5) is translated by (−6, 4, 2) and then reflected across the xy-plane. What are its final coordinates?","a": "(−4, 1, −7)","lvl": 2,"choices": ["(4, −1, 7)","(−4, −1, 7)","(−4, 1, −3)","(−4, 1, −7)"]},{"id": "MM.12-15","std": "MM.12","q": "A point P(−2, 5, −3) is rotated 180° about the x-axis and then dilated from the origin by a scale factor of 2. What are the coordinates of the final image?","a": "(−4, −10, 6)","lvl": 2,"choices": ["(−4, −10, 6)","(4, −10, 6)","(4, 10, −6)","(−4, 10, −6)"]},{"id": "MM.12-16","std": "MM.12","q": "In a right-handed 3D coordinate system, a motion-capture sensor at (3, 2, −6) is rotated 270° counterclockwise about the y-axis (when viewed from the positive y-axis toward the origin, mapping (x, y, z) to (−z, y, x)) and then translated by (1, −4, 2). What are the sensor's final coordinates?","a": "(7, −2, 5)","lvl": 3,"choices": ["(−5, −2, −1)","(7, −2, 5)","(7, 2, −1)","(−2, −2, −4)"]},{"id": "MM.12-17","std": "MM.12","q": "A 3D-printable rectangular storage box has opposite corners at (0, 0, 0) and (4, 3, 2) (in centimeters), giving a volume of 24 cm³ and a surface area of 52 cm². If the entire box is dilated from the origin by a scale factor of 2.5, what are the new volume and new surface area?","a": "Volume = 375 cm³, surface area = 325 cm²","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Volume = 60 cm³, surface area = 130 cm²","Volume = 150 cm³, surface area = 130 cm²","Volume = 375 cm³, surface area = 325 cm²","Volume = 375 cm³, surface area = 130 cm²"]},{"id": "MM.12-18","std": "MM.12","q": "A pillar has bottom corner A(−4, 0, 0) and top corner B(−4, 0, 10). It is reflected across the yz-plane, translated 6 units in the positive y-direction, then dilated from the origin by 1/2. Which result is correct?","a": "Final A(2, 3, 0) and B(2, 3, 5); the reflection and translation preserve congruence, and the dilation preserves only similarity","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Final A(−2, 3, 0) and B(−2, 3, 5); the reflection and translation preserve congruence","Final A(2, 3, 0) and B(2, 3, 5); all three steps preserve congruence","Final A(4, 6, 0) and B(4, 6, 10); the dilation preserves only similarity","Final A(2, 3, 0) and B(2, 3, 5); the reflection and translation preserve congruence, and the dilation preserves only similarity"]},{"id": "MM.13-10","std": "MM.13","q": "A museum builds a 1:25 scale model of a historic locomotive that is 50 feet long and 15 feet tall. How long is the scale model in feet?","a": "2 feet","lvl": 1,"choices": ["2 feet","0.6 feet","25 feet","1.25 feet"]},{"id": "MM.13-11","std": "MM.13","q": "A plaza monument has four identical corner lanterns arranged symmetrically around the central vertical z-axis at the same height. After designing one lantern in the first octant, which set of transformations generates the other three lanterns around the monument?","a": "Rotations of 90°, 180°, and 270° about the z-axis","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Dilations from the origin by factors 2, 3, and 4","Rotations of 90°, 180°, and 270° about the z-axis","Translations of 90, 180, and 270 units along the z-axis","Reflections across the xy-plane repeated three times"]},{"id": "MM.13-12","std": "MM.13","q": "A cylindrical municipal water tank has a measured diameter of 36 feet. Its 3D-printed scale model has a diameter of 9 inches. What is the scale ratio (model : actual) expressed in the same units?","a": "1 : 48","lvl": 1,"choices": ["1 : 4","1 : 36","1 : 48","1 : 27"]},{"id": "MM.13-13","std": "MM.13","q": "To build a 1:50 scale model of a school flagpole, a student measures the flagpole's shadow at 60 feet at the exact same time a vertical 5-foot fence post casts a 4-foot shadow. What should the flagpole's height be on the 1:50 scale model?","a": "1.5 feet","lvl": 2,"choices": ["1.2 feet","0.96 feet","3.0 feet","1.5 feet"]},{"id": "MM.13-14","std": "MM.13","q": "An architect's 1:20 scale resin model of a solid concrete monument has a volume of 150 cm³. Using 1 m³ = 1,000,000 cm³, how many cubic meters of concrete are needed to cast the full-size monument?","a": "1.2 m³","lvl": 2,"choices": ["1.2 m³","0.06 m³","0.003 m³","12.0 m³"]},{"id": "MM.13-15","std": "MM.13","q": "In a 1:100 scale model of an office tower, the bottom-left window on the front facade is centered at (2, 0, 3) cm. Identical windows are copied by translating 1.5 cm per column in the positive x-direction and 2.5 cm per floor in the positive z-direction. What are the coordinates of the center of the window that is 4 columns to the right and 3 floors above the bottom-left window?","a": "(8, 0, 10.5) cm","lvl": 2,"choices": ["(6, 0, 6) cm","(8, 0, 10.5) cm","(12, 0, 9) cm","(6.5, 0, 8) cm"]},{"id": "MM.13-16","std": "MM.13","q": "Standing 48 feet horizontally from the base of a vertical town clock tower on level ground, a student measures an angle of elevation of 53.13° (where tan(53.13°) = 4/3) from ground level to the top of the tower walls. How tall should those tower walls be in inches on a 1:32 scale model?","a": "24 inches","lvl": 3,"choices": ["18 inches","13.5 inches","24 inches","2 inches"]},{"id": "MM.13-17","std": "MM.13","q": "A full-size pedestrian bridge has a front-left steel brace connecting (−60, 0, 0) to (−20, 0, 30) (in meters). A designer reflects this brace across the yz-plane to form the front-right brace, translates a duplicate of the front-right brace 16 meters in the positive y-direction to form the back-right brace, and then dilates the entire bridge by 1/400 to create a miniature model. What are the endpoints of that back-right brace on the miniature model, expressed in centimeters (1 m = 100 cm)?","a": "(15, 4, 0) cm and (5, 4, 7.5) cm","lvl": 3,"choices": ["(−15, 4, 0) cm and (−5, 4, 7.5) cm","(15, 16, 0) cm and (5, 16, 7.5) cm","(1.5, 0.4, 0) cm and (0.5, 0.4, 0.75) cm","(15, 4, 0) cm and (5, 4, 7.5) cm"]},{"id": "MM.13-18","std": "MM.13","q": "A pavilion is 24 ft wide and 40 ft long with 10 ft posts, and its roof ridge rises 5 ft above the posts (no overhang). What is the rafter length, and what are the width, length, post height, rise, and rafter on a 1:40 model?","a": "Rafter 13 ft; model 7.2 in., 12.0 in., 3.0 in., 1.5 in., 3.9 in.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Rafter 13 ft; model 7.2 in., 12.0 in., 3.0 in., 1.5 in., 3.9 in.","Rafter 13 ft; model 0.6 in., 1.0 in., 0.25 in., 0.125 in., 0.325 in.","Rafter 13 ft; model 9.6 in., 16.0 in., 4.0 in., 2.0 in., 5.2 in.","Rafter 17 ft; model 7.2 in., 12.0 in., 3.0 in., 1.5 in., 5.1 in."]},{"id": "MM.14-10","std": "MM.14","q": "A model rocket launched straight upward has height h(t) = −16t² + 64t (in feet), where t is time in seconds after launch. What is the rocket's height at t = 2 seconds?","a": "64 feet","lvl": 1,"choices": ["128 feet","64 feet","96 feet","192 feet"]},{"id": "MM.14-11","std": "MM.14","q": "A 200 mg dose of medication decays in the bloodstream with a half-life of 4 hours, modeled by M(t) = 200 × (0.5)^{t/4}. How many milligrams remain after 12 hours?","a": "25 mg","lvl": 1,"choices": ["50 mg","66.7 mg","25 mg","12.5 mg"]},{"id": "MM.14-12","std": "MM.14","q": "Water depth at a coastal pier cycles sinusoidally every 12 hours, reaching a high tide of 14 feet and a low tide of 4 feet. What are the midline and amplitude of this periodic model?","a": "Midline = 9 feet, amplitude = 5 feet","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Midline = 10 feet, amplitude = 5 feet","Midline = 9 feet, amplitude = 10 feet","Midline = 5 feet, amplitude = 9 feet","Midline = 9 feet, amplitude = 5 feet"]},{"id": "MM.14-13","std": "MM.14","q": "A research pond begins with 400 algae cells and grows by 15% per day, modeled by P(t) = 400 × (1.15)^t. Rounded to the nearest whole cell, how many algae cells does the model predict after 6 days?","a": "925 cells","lvl": 2,"choices": ["925 cells","760 cells","805 cells","1,064 cells"]},{"id": "MM.14-14","std": "MM.14","q": "Temperature inside a greenhouse cycles daily according to T(t) = 72 − 12 × cos((π/12) × t), where t is hours after 4:00 AM. What temperatures does the model predict at 10:00 AM (t = 6) and at 4:00 PM (t = 12)?","a": "72°F at 10:00 AM and 84°F at 4:00 PM","lvl": 2,"choices": ["72°F at 10:00 AM and 60°F at 4:00 PM","72°F at 10:00 AM and 84°F at 4:00 PM","60°F at 10:00 AM and 84°F at 4:00 PM","84°F at 10:00 AM and 72°F at 4:00 PM"]},{"id": "MM.14-15","std": "MM.14","q": "A rescue drone releases a supply crate from a height of 256 feet, modeled by h(t) = 256 − 16t² (in feet, t in seconds). After how many seconds does the crate pass through a height of 112 feet?","a": "3 seconds","lvl": 2,"choices": ["4 seconds","9 seconds","3 seconds","2.65 seconds"]},{"id": "MM.14-16","std": "MM.14","q": "City A has a population of 45,000 growing exponentially at 3% per year, while City B has a population of 60,000 declining exponentially at 2% per year. After how many full years will City A's population first exceed City B's population?","a": "6 years","lvl": 3,"choices": ["4 years","5 years","7 years","6 years"]},{"id": "MM.14-17","std": "MM.14","q": "Average monthly high temperatures in a coastal town reach a minimum of 54°F in January (m = 1) and a maximum of 90°F in July (m = 7), modeled by the 12-month sinusoidal function T(m) = 72 − 18 × cos((π/6) × (m − 1)). What average high temperature does this model predict for May (m = 5)?","a": "81°F","lvl": 3,"choices": ["81°F","63°F","72°F","87.6°F"]},{"id": "MM.14-18","std": "MM.14","q": "Bread at 200°F cools in a 70°F kitchen to 135°F after 15 minutes, modeled by T(t) = 70 + 130(0.5)^{t/15}. The measured temperature at 45 minutes is 92°F. Which statement is correct?","a": "T(45) = 86.25°F; the model underestimates by 5.75°F, possibly because the kitchen air warmed above 70°F","lvl": 3,"choices": ["T(45) = 86.25°F; the model overestimates by 5.75°F","T(45) = 86.25°F; the model underestimates by 5.75°F, possibly because the kitchen air warmed above 70°F","T(45) = 102.5°F; the model overestimates by 10.5°F","T(45) = 70°F; the model underestimates by 22°F"]},{"id": "MM.15-10","std": "MM.15","part": "a","q": "Before choosing a nonlinear regression model from a scatter plot, why is it important to collect a sufficient number of data points spread across the domain?","a": "Too few points can make a curved pattern look linear or hide turning points and concavity changes","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Graphing calculators cannot plot fewer than 50 points","More data points guarantee that the relationship will become linear","Too few points can make a curved pattern look linear or hide turning points and concavity changes","Extra data points eliminate all measurement error"]},{"id": "MM.15-11","std": "MM.15","part": "b","q": "A scatter plot displays hours of typing practice (x) on the horizontal axis and number of errors per page (y) on the vertical axis. As x increases from 1 to 10, the points fall steadily along a nearly straight line from (1, 18) to (10, 2). How is the overall relationship best described?","a": "A strong decreasing linear relationship","lvl": 1,"choices": ["A strong increasing linear relationship","A concave-down quadratic relationship with an interior maximum","No relationship between the two variables","A strong decreasing linear relationship"]},{"id": "MM.15-12","std": "MM.15","part": "c","q": "A linear regression equation relating weekly advertising spend x (in hundreds of dollars) to store revenue y (in thousands of dollars) is y = 1.8x + 12.5. What weekly revenue does the model predict when §500 (x = 5) is spent on advertising?","a": "§21,500","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§21,500","§19,300","§21.50","§912,500"]},{"id": "MM.15-13","std": "MM.15","part": "b","q": "A scatter plot of highway speed x (in mph, from 20 to 75) versus fuel economy y (in mpg) passes through (20, 22), (35, 30), (50, 34), (65, 30), and (75, 25). Which description best matches the overall relationship?","a": "A nonlinear, concave-down pattern with a maximum near 50 mph","lvl": 2,"choices": ["A positive linear pattern with constant rate of change","A nonlinear, concave-down pattern with a maximum near 50 mph","A concave-up exponential decay pattern","A strictly increasing pattern with an inflection point at 50 mph"]},{"id": "MM.15-14","std": "MM.15","part": "c","q": "Using video tracking, a coach fits a quadratic regression model y = −0.02x² + 0.8x + 0.5 for the height y (in meters) of a soccer ball at horizontal distance x (in meters) from the kicker. What height does the model predict at x = 25 meters?","a": "8.0 meters","lvl": 2,"choices": ["33.0 meters","20.0 meters","8.0 meters","7.5 meters"]},{"id": "MM.15-15","std": "MM.15","part": "a","q": "An analyst wants to create a scatter plot to determine whether a new mobile game's active player count over its first year follows exponential growth or an S-shaped logistic curve that levels off. Which data plan is best?","a": "Record active player counts at regular intervals across all 12 months rather than sampling only the first 3 weeks","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Plot only the launch day and Day 365 to connect them with a curve","Record 100 measurements all taken during the first 48 hours after launch","Remove any months where player growth begins to slow down","Record active player counts at regular intervals across all 12 months rather than sampling only the first 3 weeks"]},{"id": "MM.15-16","std": "MM.15","part": "b","q": "A logistic regression curve modeling the number of students who have heard an announcement at a school of 800 students increases slowly at first (concave up), passes through (4, 400) where the rate of increase reaches its peak, and then increases more slowly approaching 800 (concave down). What feature occurs at (4, 400)?","a": "An inflection point where the curve changes from concave up to concave down","lvl": 3,"choices": ["An inflection point where the curve changes from concave up to concave down","A global maximum where the number of students begins decreasing","A global minimum of the logistic function","A vertical asymptote where the model is undefined"]},{"id": "MM.15-17","std": "MM.15","part": "c","q": "A linear regression model y = −0.065x + 92 fits laptop battery health percentage y against charge cycles x using test data collected between x = 100 and x = 500 cycles. When a student substitutes x = 1,600 cycles, the model predicts −12% battery health. What best explains this nonsensical result?","a": "Dangerous extrapolation far outside the observed data range of 100 to 500 cycles","lvl": 3,"choices": ["A sign error when multiplying −0.065 by 1,600","Dangerous extrapolation far outside the observed data range of 100 to 500 cycles","Battery health must actually increase after 500 charge cycles","The regression line had too many data points between 100 and 500"]},{"id": "MM.15-18","std": "MM.15","part": "a","q": "A seedling's height (cm) at week x is (0, 2), (2, 5), (4, 14), (6, 30), (8, 46), (10, 55), (12, 58). Which description and week-14 prediction are best?","a": "Increasing; concave up until about week 6, then concave down; inflection near (6, 30); levels off near 60 cm; about 59-60 cm at week 14","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Increasing linearly at about 4.7 cm per week; about 67 cm at week 14","Quadratic with a maximum at week 12; about 55 cm at week 14","Increasing; concave up until about week 6, then concave down; inflection near (6, 30); levels off near 60 cm; about 59-60 cm at week 14","Exponential growth; about 100 cm at week 14"]},{"id": "MM.16-10","std": "MM.16","q": "Which logarithmic transformation converts an exponential relationship y = a × b^x into a linear relationship?","a": "Plotting (x, log(y)) by taking the logarithm of y only","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Plotting (log(x), y) by taking the logarithm of x only","Plotting (log(x), log(y)) by taking the logarithm of both variables","Plotting (x, y²) by squaring the response variable","Plotting (x, log(y)) by taking the logarithm of y only"]},{"id": "MM.16-11","std": "MM.16","q": "Which logarithmic transformation converts a power-law relationship y = a × x^p into a linear relationship?","a": "Plotting (log(x), log(y)) by taking the logarithm of both x and y","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Plotting (log(x), log(y)) by taking the logarithm of both x and y","Plotting (x, log(y)) by taking the logarithm of y only","Plotting (log(x), y) by taking the logarithm of x only","Plotting (x², y) by squaring x"]},{"id": "MM.16-12","std": "MM.16","q": "After linearizing exponential data with base-10 logarithms, a student obtains the regression line log(y) = 0.3010x + 2. What is the initial value a (at x = 0) in the original exponential model y = a × b^x?","a": "100","lvl": 1,"choices": ["2","100","20","0.3010"]},{"id": "MM.16-13","std": "MM.16","q": "Linearizing a set of nonlinear data with base-10 logarithms produces the linear model log(y) = 0.2x + 1.4. What value of y does this model predict when x = 3?","a": "100","lvl": 2,"choices": ["2","20","100","25.1"]},{"id": "MM.16-14","std": "MM.16","q": "A student linearizes growth data using natural logarithms and finds the line of best fit ln(y) = 0.45x + 3.2. Rewriting this in the exponential form y = a × e^{kx}, what are the values of a (rounded to the nearest tenth) and k?","a": "a = 24.5 and k = 0.45","lvl": 2,"choices": ["a = 3.2 and k = 0.45","a = 24.5 and k = 1.57","a = 1,584.9 and k = 0.45","a = 24.5 and k = 0.45"]},{"id": "MM.16-15","std": "MM.16","q": "An astronomy student takes base-10 logarithms of planetary orbital period T (in years) and orbital distance r (in AU) and finds the linear model log(T) = 1.5 × log(r). Which nonlinear power equation relates T and r?","a": "T = r^1.5","lvl": 2,"choices": ["T = r^1.5","T = 1.5 × r","T = 1.5^r","T = 10^{1.5r}"]},{"id": "MM.16-16","std": "MM.16","q": "When a bivariate dataset (x, y) is transformed using base-10 logarithms, the points (x, log(y)) lie exactly on a straight line passing through (0, 1.0), (2, 2.0), and (4, 3.0). Which exponential model y = a × b^x represents the original data (with b rounded to the nearest hundredth)?","a": "y = 10 × (3.16)^x","lvl": 3,"choices": ["y = 1 × (0.50)^x","y = 10 × (3.16)^x","y = 10 × (0.50)^x","y = 3.16 × (10)^x"]},{"id": "MM.16-17","std": "MM.16","q": "A biologist tests two log transformations on mammal body mass M and metabolic rate R. Plotting (M, log(R)) leaves a strong curve in the residuals (r² = 0.78), while plotting (log(M), log(R)) produces random residuals (r² = 0.99) with base-10 regression line log(R) = 0.75 × log(M) + 1.85. Which model best fits the data, and by what factor does R increase if M is multiplied by 16?","a": "Power model R = 70.8 × M^0.75; multiplying M by 16 multiplies R by 8","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Exponential model R = 70.8 × (5.62)^M; multiplying M by 16 multiplies R by 12","Power model R = 1.85 × M^0.75; multiplying M by 16 multiplies R by 12","Power model R = 70.8 × M^0.75; multiplying M by 16 multiplies R by 8","Power model R = 70.8 × M^0.75; multiplying M by 16 multiplies R by 16"]},{"id": "MM.16-18","std": "MM.16","q": "Bacteria counts are (0, 50), (1, 150), (2, 450), (3, 1350), (4, 4050). Linearize with base-10 logarithms. Which result is correct?","a": "log(y) = 0.4771x + 1.6990, so a = 50 and b = 3; y = 36,450 at x = 6","lvl": 3,"choices": ["log(y) = 0.4771x + 1.6990, so a = 1.699 and b = 0.4771","log(y) = 3x + 50, so y = 68 at x = 6","log(y) = 0.4771x + 1.6990, so y = 12,150 at x = 6","log(y) = 0.4771x + 1.6990, so a = 50 and b = 3; y = 36,450 at x = 6"]},{"id": "MM.17-10","std": "MM.17","q": "What are the four stages of the Statistical Problem-Solving Cycle in the standard order?","a": "Formulate statistical investigative questions, Collect/consider data, Analyze data, Interpret results","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Formulate statistical investigative questions, Collect/consider data, Analyze data, Interpret results","Collect data, Prove a hypothesis, Graph residuals, Publish conclusions","Interpret results, Collect data, Formulate questions, Analyze data","Formulate questions, Interpret results, Analyze data, Collect data"]},{"id": "MM.17-11","std": "MM.17","q": "Which of the following is a valid statistical investigative question because it anticipates variability in the data?","a": "How many minutes per night do eleventh graders at our school spend on homework?","lvl": 1,"choices": ["How many minutes are in one hour?","How many minutes per night do eleventh graders at our school spend on homework?","What year was our high school building opened?","How many pages are in our mathematics textbook?"]},{"id": "MM.17-12","std": "MM.17","q": "A student wants to determine whether listening to quiet instrumental music causes higher scores on a vocabulary memory quiz. Which data collection design can legitimately establish cause and effect?","a": "A randomized controlled experiment randomly assigning participants to music or silence groups","lvl": 1,"choices": ["An online poll asking students whether they think music helps them study","An observational survey comparing students who already study with music to those who do not","A randomized controlled experiment randomly assigning participants to music or silence groups","Measuring only a single student taking the quiz with music playing"]},{"id": "MM.17-13","std": "MM.17","q": "A cafeteria director wants to know which new lunch entree the entire student body prefers. She surveys the first 40 students who walk into 1st lunch period. Which stage of the Statistical Problem-Solving Cycle is flawed, and why?","a": "Collect/consider data, because a convenience sample from 1st lunch alone may not represent all students","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Formulate questions, because lunch preference is not a statistical question","Analyze data, because 40 responses cannot be turned into percentages","Interpret results, because categorical variables cannot be summarized","Collect/consider data, because a convenience sample from 1st lunch alone may not represent all students"]},{"id": "MM.17-14","std": "MM.17","q": "In a randomized experiment comparing two exam-review methods among 60 volunteers, Method A yields mean score 84 (SD 5) and Method B yields mean score 79 (SD 6), with p = 0.018. Which statement belongs properly in the 'Interpret results' stage?","a": "There is convincing evidence that Method A causes higher average quiz scores than Method B for students like those in the study","lvl": 2,"choices": ["There is convincing evidence that Method A causes higher average quiz scores than Method B for students like those in the study","Every single student who uses Method A will score exactly 5 points higher than with Method B","Because p = 0.018, the difference is guaranteed to be caused by random chance","Method A should be required for every student in the nation because 60 volunteers represent all students"]},{"id": "MM.17-15","std": "MM.17","q": "A city planner investigates whether new downtown protected bike lanes increased ridership. She counts cyclists on one sunny Saturday before installation and one rainy Tuesday after installation. What is the main flaw in her data collection?","a": "Weather and day of the week are confounded with the bike-lane installation","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Cyclist counts cannot be analyzed statistically","Weather and day of the week are confounded with the bike-lane installation","She should have used a pie chart instead of counting cyclists","Counting cyclists on a Tuesday introduces voluntary response bias"]},{"id": "MM.17-16","std": "MM.17","q": "A student investigates morning tardiness by checking attendance records for a random sample of 60 bus riders (3 tardies) and 60 car riders (12 tardies) at her school. Which interpretation is statistically sound?","a": "Car riders had a 20% tardy rate versus 5% for bus riders, indicating an association at the school, but this observational study cannot prove that riding in a car causes tardiness","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Riding in a car directly causes a 15 percentage point increase in tardiness for all high schoolers","Because 12 is greater than 3, every car rider is 4 times more likely to be tardy regardless of confounding factors","Car riders had a 20% tardy rate versus 5% for bus riders, indicating an association at the school, but this observational study cannot prove that riding in a car causes tardiness","The study is invalid because 60 students per group is too small to calculate proportions"]},{"id": "MM.17-17","std": "MM.17","q": "During the 'Analyze data' stage of a study on daily screen time among 50 randomly sampled teens, a histogram shows a strong right skew with three extreme outliers above 10 hours per day. What is the best analytical practice before interpreting the results?","a": "Verify whether the three extreme values are accurate or recording errors, report median and IQR alongside mean and SD, and explain how the skew affects the mean","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Automatically delete the three highest values so the histogram looks symmetric","Report only the mean because it uses every data value and ignores skew","Replace the outliers with the sample median without mentioning the change","Verify whether the three extreme values are accurate or recording errors, report median and IQR alongside mean and SD, and explain how the skew affects the mean"]},{"id": "MM.17-18","std": "MM.17","q": "An athletic director asks whether a 15-minute guided stretching warm-up reduces hamstring soreness among 60 track athletes. Which plan follows all four stages of the Statistical Problem-Solving Cycle?","a": "Ask whether the warm-up lowers average soreness; randomly assign athletes within event groups; compare boxplots and means with a test; conclude only for similar track athletes","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Ask whether the warm-up lowers average soreness; randomly assign athletes within event groups; compare boxplots and means with a test; conclude only for similar track athletes","Survey athletes about whether they like stretching and report the percentage","Let athletes choose their warm-up and conclude for all athletes in every sport","Randomly assign athletes, and if p < 0.05, conclude stretching prevents all injuries"]},{"id": "MM.18-10","std": "MM.18","part": "a","q": "A bakery tracks the number of pastries X purchased per customer across 200 orders: 0 pastries (50 orders), 1 pastry (90 orders), 2 pastries (44 orders), and 3 pastries (16 orders). Based on these empirical observations, what is the estimated probability P(X = 2)?","a": "0.22","lvl": 1,"choices": ["0.44","0.22","0.30","0.45"]},{"id": "MM.18-11","std": "MM.18","part": "b","q": "A relative frequency histogram of customer checkout times has four consecutive bars with heights 0.15, 0.35, 0.30, and 0.20. What is the cumulative relative frequency at the upper boundary of the second interval?","a": "0.50","lvl": 1,"choices": ["0.35","0.80","0.50","0.20"]},{"id": "MM.18-12","std": "MM.18","part": "c","q": "An empirical probability distribution of household incomes in a county is strongly skewed to the right with several very high outliers. Which pair of measures is most appropriate to summarize its center and spread?","a": "Median and interquartile range (IQR)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Mean and standard deviation","Mean and range","Mode and standard deviation","Median and interquartile range (IQR)"]},{"id": "MM.18-13","std": "MM.18","part": "a","q": "A computer simulation of a carnival prize wheel runs 500 trials and records payouts: §0 (210 times), §5 (165 times), §10 (85 times), and §20 (40 times). What is the empirical probability of winning at least §10 on a single spin?","a": "0.25","lvl": 2,"choices": ["0.25","0.17","0.08","0.58"]},{"id": "MM.18-14","std": "MM.18","part": "b","q": "A cumulative relative frequency graph of pizza delivery times (in minutes) passes through (10, 0.10), (20, 0.35), (30, 0.75), (40, 0.92), and (50, 1.00). What proportion of deliveries took more than 20 minutes and at most 40 minutes?","a": "0.57","lvl": 2,"choices": ["0.92","0.57","0.40","0.65"]},{"id": "MM.18-15","std": "MM.18","part": "c","q": "An empirical probability distribution for the number of defective LED bulbs X in a shipping carton is: X = 0 (P = 0.60), X = 1 (P = 0.25), X = 2 (P = 0.10), X = 3 (P = 0.05). What is the mean (expected value) number of defective bulbs per carton?","a": "0.60 bulbs","lvl": 2,"choices": ["1.50 bulbs","0.25 bulbs","0.60 bulbs","1.00 bulb"]},{"id": "MM.18-16","std": "MM.18","part": "c","q": "A help desk models the number of urgent calls per hour X with the empirical probability distribution: X = 0 (P = 0.20), X = 1 (P = 0.40), X = 2 (P = 0.30), X = 3 (P = 0.10). What are the mean and standard deviation of X?","a": "Mean = 1.30 calls, standard deviation = 0.90 calls","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Mean = 1.50 calls, standard deviation = 0.90 calls","Mean = 1.30 calls, standard deviation = 0.81 calls","Mean = 1.30 calls, standard deviation = 1.12 calls","Mean = 1.30 calls, standard deviation = 0.90 calls"]},{"id": "MM.18-17","std": "MM.18","part": "b","q": "A cumulative relative frequency graph of student quiz scores passes through the points (50, 0.05), (60, 0.15), (70, 0.25), (80, 0.50), (90, 0.75), and (100, 1.00). Based on this graph, what are the median and interquartile range (IQR) of the distribution?","a": "Median = 80, IQR = 20","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Median = 80, IQR = 20","Median = 75, IQR = 25","Median = 80, IQR = 50","Median = 80, IQR = 10"]},{"id": "MM.18-18","std": "MM.18","part": "a","q": "A food bank needs X boxes per day: 10 (10 days), 15 (20 days), 20 (40 days), 25 (20 days), and 30 (10 days) over 100 days. Which summary is correct?","a": "Mean 20, SD ≈ 5.48, median 20, IQR 10; the distribution is symmetric, so mean and SD fit; about 600 boxes in 30 days","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Mean 20, SD 30, median 20, IQR 10; about 600 boxes in 30 days","Mean 20, SD ≈ 5.48, median 20, IQR 10; the distribution is symmetric, so mean and SD fit; about 600 boxes in 30 days","Mean 20, SD ≈ 5.48, median 20, IQR 15; about 20 boxes in 30 days","Mean 22, SD ≈ 5.48, median 20, IQR 10; about 660 boxes in 30 days"]},{"id": "MM.19-10","std": "MM.19","part": "a","q": "A basketball player makes 80% of her free throws independently. Using the binomial formula P(X = k) = C(n, k) × p^k × (1 − p)^{n − k}, what is the probability she makes exactly 3 out of 4 free throws?","a": "0.4096","lvl": 1,"choices": ["0.1024","0.5120","0.4096","0.7500"]},{"id": "MM.19-11","std": "MM.19","part": "b","q": "Which Large Counts condition must be satisfied before using a normal distribution to approximate the sampling distribution of a sample proportion p̂ from a random sample of size n and population proportion p?","a": "Both n × p ≥ 10 and n × (1 − p) ≥ 10","lvl": 1,"choices": ["n ≥ 10 regardless of the value of p","p must equal exactly 0.50","n × p ≤ 10 and n × (1 − p) ≤ 10","Both n × p ≥ 10 and n × (1 − p) ≥ 10"]},{"id": "MM.19-12","std": "MM.19","part": "c","q": "A population of shipped parcel weights has mean 12 pounds and standard deviation 4 pounds. For random samples of size n = 64 parcels, what are the mean and standard deviation of the sampling distribution of the sample mean?","a": "Mean = 12 pounds, standard deviation = 0.5 pounds","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Mean = 12 pounds, standard deviation = 0.5 pounds","Mean = 12 pounds, standard deviation = 4 pounds","Mean = 1.5 pounds, standard deviation = 0.5 pounds","Mean = 12 pounds, standard deviation = 0.0625 pounds"]},{"id": "MM.19-13","std": "MM.19","part": "a","q": "In a town where 60% of registered voters support a park bond (p = 0.60), a student selects a random sample of n = 5 voters. Using the binomial distribution, what is the probability that at least 4 of the 5 voters support the bond, rounded to four decimal places?","a": "0.3370","lvl": 2,"choices": ["0.2592","0.3370","0.0778","0.6630"]},{"id": "MM.19-14","std": "MM.19","part": "b","q": "In a large state, 40% of licensed drivers own an SUV (p = 0.40). For a random sample of n = 150 drivers, the Large Counts condition is met (np = 60 and n(1 − p) = 90). What are the mean and standard deviation of the normal sampling distribution for the sample proportion p̂?","a": "Mean = 0.40, standard deviation = 0.04","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Mean = 0.40, standard deviation = 0.0016","Mean = 60, standard deviation = 0.04","Mean = 0.40, standard deviation = 0.04","Mean = 0.40, standard deviation = 0.24"]},{"id": "MM.19-15","std": "MM.19","part": "d","q": "For random bags of n = 25 orchard apples, the sampling distribution of the sample mean apple weight is normal with mean 180 grams and standard deviation 3 grams. Using the Empirical Rule (68-95-99.7), what is the approximate long-term probability that a random bag of 25 apples has a mean weight between 174 grams and 186 grams?","a": "0.95","lvl": 2,"choices": ["0.68","0.997","0.475","0.95"]},{"id": "MM.19-16","std": "MM.19","part": "c","q": "Patient wait times at a walk-in clinic are strongly right-skewed with population mean 24 minutes and standard deviation 12 minutes. By the Central Limit Theorem for random samples of n = 36 patients, what is the approximate probability that the sample mean wait time exceeds 28 minutes? (Use P(Z > 2) = 0.0228.)","a": "0.0228","lvl": 3,"choices": ["0.0228","0.9772","0.3694","0.1587"]},{"id": "MM.19-17","std": "MM.19","part": "d","q": "A cereal maker places a prize code in 20% of all boxes (p = 0.20). For a random shipment of n = 400 boxes, the sample proportion p̂ is approximately normal with mean 0.20 and standard deviation 0.02. Using P(Z < −1) = 0.1587 and P(Z < 2) = 0.9772, what is the long-term probability that between 18% and 24% of boxes in a random shipment contain a prize code?","a": "0.8185","lvl": 3,"choices": ["0.9772","0.8185","0.6826","0.1815"]},{"id": "MM.19-18","std": "MM.19","part": "c","q": "Purchase totals are right-skewed with mean §48 and SD §30. For random samples of 100 customers, which statement is correct? (P(Z > 2) = 0.0228)","a": "The sampling distribution is approximately normal with mean §48 and SD §3; P(sample mean ≥ §54) = 0.0228; with n = 400 the SD drops to §1.50","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Mean §48 and SD §30; P(sample mean ≥ §54) = 0.0228; the SD stays §30 for n = 400","Mean §48 and SD §3; P(sample mean ≥ §54) = 0.9772; with n = 400 the SD drops to §0.75","The sampling distribution is approximately normal with mean §48 and SD §3; P(sample mean ≥ §54) = 0.0228; with n = 400 the SD drops to §1.50","The sampling distribution stays right-skewed because the population is skewed, so no probability can be found"]},{"id": "MM.20-10","std": "MM.20","part": "a","q": "A random survey of registered voters finds a sample proportion p̂ = 0.58 supporting a county library levy, with a margin of error of 0.04 at the 95% confidence level. What is the 95% confidence interval for the population proportion?","a": "(0.54, 0.62)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["(0.56, 0.60)","(0.50, 0.66)","(0.58, 0.62)","(0.54, 0.62)"]},{"id": "MM.20-11","std": "MM.20","part": "c","q": "A cereal manufacturer prints a net weight of 16 ounces on its boxes. A consumer group suspects the boxes are systematically underfilled (contain less than 16 ounces on average). What are the appropriate null and alternative hypotheses?","a": "H0: μ = 16, Ha: μ < 16","lvl": 1,"choices": ["H0: μ = 16, Ha: μ < 16","H0: μ < 16, Ha: μ = 16","H0: μ = 16, Ha: μ > 16","H0: μ = 16, Ha: μ ≠ 16"]},{"id": "MM.20-12","std": "MM.20","part": "d","q": "A significance test is conducted at significance level alpha = 0.05 and yields a p-value of 0.014. What is the correct statistical decision?","a": "Reject H0, because the p-value (0.014) is less than alpha (0.05)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Fail to reject H0, because 0.014 is less than 0.05","Reject H0, because the p-value (0.014) is less than alpha (0.05)","Accept H0 as proven true","Increase alpha to 0.10 so H0 does not have to be rejected"]},{"id": "MM.20-13","std": "MM.20","part": "a","q": "A random sample of n = 64 college textbooks has a sample mean price of §112 and a sample standard deviation of §16. Using critical value z = 1.96, what is the 95% confidence interval for the population mean textbook price?","a": "(§108.08, §115.92)","lvl": 2,"choices": ["(§110.00, §114.00)","(§80.64, §143.36)","(§108.08, §115.92)","(§111.51, §112.49)"]},{"id": "MM.20-14","std": "MM.20","part": "b","q": "A random survey produces a 95% confidence interval of (0.53, 0.63) for the proportion of town residents who favor extending public library evening hours. The town council will approve funding only if there is convincing evidence that more than 50% of residents favor the extension. What strategic decision is supported?","a": "Approve funding, because the entire 95% confidence interval lies above 0.50","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Reject funding, because the interval does not reach 1.00","Reject funding, because 0.53 is too close to 0.50","Delay voting, because a 95% confidence interval cannot be compared to 0.50","Approve funding, because the entire 95% confidence interval lies above 0.50"]},{"id": "MM.20-15","std": "MM.20","part": "c","q": "A battery maker claims its AA batteries last an average of 40 hours (H0: μ = 40, Ha: μ ≠ 40). A random sample of n = 36 batteries has a mean lifetime of 38.5 hours and a sample standard deviation of 3.6 hours. What is the test statistic t = (x̄ − μ0) / (s / √n)?","a": "−2.50","lvl": 2,"choices": ["−2.50","−0.42","2.50","−15.00"]},{"id": "MM.20-16","std": "MM.20","part": "b","q": "A coffee subscription service surveys a random sample of 400 members and finds 260 prefer dark roast (p̂ = 0.65). Using z = 1.96, what is the 95% confidence interval (rounded to three decimal places) and its correct interpretation?","a": "(0.603, 0.697); we are 95% confident that the true proportion of all members who prefer dark roast is between 0.603 and 0.697","lvl": 3,"choices": ["(0.626, 0.674); 95% of all members prefer dark roast between 62.6% and 67.4% of the time","(0.603, 0.697); we are 95% confident that the true proportion of all members who prefer dark roast is between 0.603 and 0.697","(0.603, 0.697); there is a 95% probability that a randomly chosen member's preference lies between 0.603 and 0.697","(0.611, 0.689); 95% of future samples will have p̂ exactly equal to 0.65"]},{"id": "MM.20-17","std": "MM.20","part": "d","q": "A pharmaceutical lab tests each medicine batch under H0: the batch meets purity standards (safe) versus Ha: the batch is contaminated (unsafe). What is a Type II error in this context, and why might safety regulators choose a higher significance level such as alpha = 0.10 instead of 0.01?","a": "A Type II error is failing to detect a contaminated batch (releasing unsafe medicine); raising alpha lowers the probability of a Type II error","lvl": 3,"choices": ["A Type II error is discarding a safe batch of medicine; raising alpha lowers the probability of discarding safe batches","A Type II error is failing to detect a contaminated batch (releasing unsafe medicine); raising alpha increases the probability of a Type II error","A Type II error is failing to detect a contaminated batch (releasing unsafe medicine); raising alpha lowers the probability of a Type II error","A Type II error is making a calculation mistake in the z-score; raising alpha eliminates arithmetic errors"]},{"id": "MM.20-18","std": "MM.20","part": "c","q": "A district claims at least 75% of buses arrive on time (H0: p = 0.75, Ha: p < 0.75). In 200 arrivals, 138 are on time. Which test result is correct? (P(Z < −1.96) = 0.025, alpha = 0.05)","a": "np0 = 150 and n(1 − p0) = 50 are both at least 10; z = −1.96; p = 0.025 < 0.05, so reject H0: fewer than 75% arrive on time","lvl": 3,"choices": ["z = −1.96; p = 0.025, so fail to reject H0","z = −0.06; the p-value is large, so fail to reject H0","The conditions fail because 138 is less than 150, so no test can be run","np0 = 150 and n(1 − p0) = 50 are both at least 10; z = −1.96; p = 0.025 < 0.05, so reject H0: fewer than 75% arrive on time"]},{"id": "MM.21-10","std": "MM.21","q": "A local radio host asks listeners to call in and vote on whether the town should fund a new skate park, and 82% of callers vote 'No.' Which source of bias most seriously weakens this survey's conclusion?","a": "Voluntary response bias, because people with strong negative opinions are far more likely to call in","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Voluntary response bias, because people with strong negative opinions are far more likely to call in","Placebo effect, because listeners knew the host's name","Rounding error from reporting 82% as a whole number","Central Limit Theorem failure because 82% is above 50%"]},{"id": "MM.21-11","std": "MM.21","q": "After a 10-year observational study finds that adults who eat dark chocolate daily have lower rates of heart disease, a website publishes the headline: 'Eating Dark Chocolate Prevents Heart Disease!' What is the primary flaw in this claim?","a": "Claiming causation from an observational study that cannot rule out confounding variables such as diet, income, or exercise","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Using a 10-year time span, which is too long for statistical analysis","Claiming causation from an observational study that cannot rule out confounding variables such as diet, income, or exercise","Failing to use a bar graph in the article","Measuring heart disease as a categorical variable"]},{"id": "MM.21-12","std": "MM.21","q": "A political group mails a survey asking: 'Do you agree that the city should stop wasting taxpayer dollars on overpriced downtown statues?' What type of bias does this wording create?","a": "Response bias caused by leading, loaded question wording","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Random sampling error","Blinding bias","Response bias caused by leading, loaded question wording","Undercoverage bias from mailing surveys"]},{"id": "MM.21-13","std": "MM.21","q": "A polling firm randomly dials 1,000 phone numbers from a landline telephone directory to estimate smartphone app usage among all city adults. What type of bias arises because adults who use only cell phones have zero chance of being selected?","a": "Undercoverage bias","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Nonresponse bias","Response bias","Placebo bias","Undercoverage bias"]},{"id": "MM.21-14","std": "MM.21","q": "A fitness magazine tests 20 different herbal teas for speeding up marathon recovery using alpha = 0.05 for each test. Only 1 tea achieves p = 0.04, and the magazine publishes a glowing report on that tea while hiding the 19 non-significant tests. Why should readers distrust this report?","a": "With 20 tests at alpha = 0.05, about 1 false positive (Type I error) is expected by random chance alone even if none of the teas work","lvl": 2,"choices": ["With 20 tests at alpha = 0.05, about 1 false positive (Type I error) is expected by random chance alone even if none of the teas work","A p-value of 0.04 means the tea is 96% guaranteed to cure muscle fatigue","Testing 20 different teas makes the p-value automatically drop to 0.00","Herbal teas cannot be evaluated with hypothesis tests"]},{"id": "MM.21-15","std": "MM.21","q": "A software company emails a satisfaction survey to 2,000 randomly chosen customers. Only 140 customers reply (a 7% response rate), and 90% of those 140 report being frustrated. Why is it invalid to conclude that 90% of all customers are frustrated?","a": "Severe nonresponse bias, because the 93% who did not reply likely hold different views than the dissatisfied users motivated to respond","lvl": 2,"choices": ["The original sample of 2,000 was not chosen randomly","Severe nonresponse bias, because the 93% who did not reply likely hold different views than the dissatisfied users motivated to respond","140 responses is too small to calculate a percentage","Email surveys always overestimate customer satisfaction"]},{"id": "MM.21-16","std": "MM.21","q": "A double-blind trial with 10,000 patients finds that a new headache pill shortens average headache duration by 2 minutes compared to a placebo (p = 0.002), while costing §40 more per bottle. Which critique best evaluates an ad calling the pill a 'major medical breakthrough'?","a": "The huge sample size gives strong statistical significance (low random error), but a 2-minute difference for §40 lacks practical significance","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Because p = 0.002 is tiny, the effect must be large and practically valuable for patients","A sample of 10,000 patients introduces voluntary response bias that invalidates p = 0.002","The huge sample size gives strong statistical significance (low random error), but a 2-minute difference for §40 lacks practical significance","Double-blind trials cannot establish statistical significance"]},{"id": "MM.21-17","std": "MM.21","q": "To estimate citywide voter support for a transit bond, Poll A uses an open online website poll with 15,000 self-selected responses, while Poll B uses a simple random sample of 600 registered voters with high response rates. Which comparison of bias and random error is accurate?","a": "Poll B is far more valid: although n = 600 has measurable random sampling error, Poll A suffers from severe selection bias that a huge sample size cannot eliminate","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Poll A is far more accurate because 15,000 responses eliminate both bias and random error","Both polls have identical validity because both measure the same transit bond","Poll B has zero random error because it uses simple random sampling","Poll B is far more valid: although n = 600 has measurable random sampling error, Poll A suffers from severe selection bias that a huge sample size cannot eliminate"]},{"id": "MM.21-18","std": "MM.21","q": "A blog says, 'Study Proves Standing Desks Boost High School GPAs!' A desk maker gave desks to 30 volunteer honors students at one private academy and compared them to 30 non-volunteers in standard classes (0.4 higher GPA, p = 0.04). Which critique is correct?","a": "Volunteers in honors classes differ from standard-class students, one private school is not representative, and the desk maker funded it; a small p-value cannot fix a biased design, so no causal claim is justified","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Volunteers in honors classes differ from standard-class students, one private school is not representative, and the desk maker funded it; a small p-value cannot fix a biased design, so no causal claim is justified","Because p = 0.04, the study proves standing desks raise GPAs","The only problem is that 60 students is too small a sample","The study is valid because GPA is a quantitative variable"]},{"id": "MM.22-10","std": "MM.22","q": "A student wants a simple random sample (SRS) of 40 students from a high school roster of 800 students. Which method produces a true SRS?","a": "Number all 800 students from 1 to 800 and use a random number generator to pick 40 distinct numbers without replacement","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Stand outside the gym before first period and ask the first 40 students who walk by","Number all 800 students from 1 to 800 and use a random number generator to pick 40 distinct numbers without replacement","Ask 40 friends from two math classes to fill out the survey","Post a QR code in the cafeteria and use the first 40 responses"]},{"id": "MM.22-11","std": "MM.22","q": "In a randomized experiment testing whether printing vocabulary quizzes on yellow paper instead of white paper improves recall, what is the primary purpose of randomly assigning students to the two paper colors?","a": "To balance known and unknown confounding variables (such as prior vocabulary skill) roughly equally across both groups","lvl": 1,"choices": ["To ensure that both groups achieve identical average scores","To allow the results to be generalized automatically to every student in the world","To balance known and unknown confounding variables (such as prior vocabulary skill) roughly equally across both groups","To make the sample size larger without recruiting more students"]},{"id": "MM.22-12","std": "MM.22","q": "A student conducts a randomized taste test comparing Brand A and Brand B sparkling water by serving both in identical cups labeled only 'X' and 'Y' in random order. Which experimental design principle is being applied?","a": "Blinding, so brand expectations do not bias taste ratings","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Stratified sampling","Cluster sampling","Extrapolation","Blinding, so brand expectations do not bias taste ratings"]},{"id": "MM.22-13","std": "MM.22","q": "A high school has 300 freshmen, 250 sophomores, 250 juniors, and 200 seniors (1,000 students total). A student wants a proportional stratified random sample of 100 students to survey about pep rallies. How many students should be randomly selected from each grade?","a": "30 freshmen, 25 sophomores, 25 juniors, and 20 seniors","lvl": 2,"choices": ["30 freshmen, 25 sophomores, 25 juniors, and 20 seniors","25 freshmen, 25 sophomores, 25 juniors, and 25 seniors","40 freshmen, 30 sophomores, 20 juniors, and 10 seniors","30 freshmen, 20 sophomores, 20 juniors, and 30 seniors"]},{"id": "MM.22-14","std": "MM.22","q": "A student conducts a matched-pairs experiment with 20 volunteers to compare typing speed (words per minute) on a laptop keyboard versus a tablet keyboard. How should randomization be implemented?","a": "Have every volunteer type on both keyboards, flipping a coin for each volunteer to randomly determine which keyboard they use first","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Have all 20 volunteers type on the laptop first and the tablet second so the procedure is uniform","Have every volunteer type on both keyboards, flipping a coin for each volunteer to randomly determine which keyboard they use first","Let each volunteer choose whichever keyboard they prefer","Put the 10 fastest typists on the laptop and the 10 slowest typists on the tablet"]},{"id": "MM.22-15","std": "MM.22","q": "In a randomized experiment with 40 student volunteers, 20 are randomly assigned to study with flashcards (mean score 86) and 20 to re-read notes (mean score 78), yielding p = 0.012. What is the proper scope of inference for this study?","a": "Cause and effect can be inferred for students similar to these 40 volunteers, but results cannot be automatically generalized to all U.S. high schoolers","lvl": 2,"choices": ["No causal conclusion is possible because volunteers were used","Cause and effect can be generalized to every high school student in the country","Cause and effect can be inferred for students similar to these 40 volunteers, but results cannot be automatically generalized to all U.S. high schoolers","Neither causation nor generalization is justified because 40 is less than 100"]},{"id": "MM.22-16","std": "MM.22","q": "A student tests whether a new organic fertilizer increases tomato yield using 24 potted tomato plants: 12 sit on a bright south windowsill and 12 sit on a shadier east windowsill. Which experimental design best controls for sunlight differences?","a": "A randomized block design blocking by windowsill, randomly assigning 6 plants on each sill to the fertilizer and 6 to the control","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Give fertilizer to all 12 south-sill plants and no fertilizer to all 12 east-sill plants","Put all 24 plants in a single list and assign the first 12 to fertilizer without randomization","Discard the 12 east-sill plants and test only 6 south-sill plants with no control group","A randomized block design blocking by windowsill, randomly assigning 6 plants on each sill to the fertilizer and 6 to the control"]},{"id": "MM.22-17","std": "MM.22","q": "A student selects a simple random sample of 120 students from her high school's full roster (with 100% response) and finds 72 prefer starting school 30 minutes later (p̂ = 0.60, 95% confidence interval: 0.512 to 0.688). Which conclusion is statistically valid?","a": "Because students were randomly selected from the school roster and the 95% confidence interval lies entirely above 0.50, there is convincing evidence that a majority of all students at her school prefer a later start time","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Because students were randomly selected from the school roster and the 95% confidence interval lies entirely above 0.50, there is convincing evidence that a majority of all students at her school prefer a later start time","Starting school 30 minutes later causes students at her school to earn higher grades","Exactly 60% of all students in the state prefer a later school start time","The survey proves 0% of students oppose a later start time"]},{"id": "MM.22-18","std": "MM.22","q": "Design an experiment on whether chewing mint gum during a 10-minute math fluency quiz improves scores for 50 volunteers. Which plan is correct?","a": "Explanatory: gum or no gum; response: quiz score; randomly assign 25 of 50 numbered volunteers to gum; use the same quiz, room, and blind grading; if p < 0.05, conclude gum causes higher scores for students like these volunteers","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Let students choose whether to chew gum, then conclude the result for all students","Explanatory: gum or no gum; response: quiz score; randomly assign 25 of 50 numbered volunteers to gum; use the same quiz, room, and blind grading; if p < 0.05, conclude gum causes higher scores for students like these volunteers","Explanatory: quiz score; response: gum or no gum; randomly assign volunteers","Randomly assign volunteers, and if p < 0.05, conclude gum raises scores for every student everywhere"]}]};
